"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nWhen did this person come? Which day?\" [1283] \"Let me think. Four-five\u2014about seven weeks ago. Oh, I remember now. It was early in the morning, on that day\u2014May 6th.\" [1284] \"May 6th!\" Pechins shouted. \"Dad, Reen sir, did you hear that?\" [1285] \"I heard it, Pett,\" said Mr. Rowe displeasedly. \"You're making it sound like March 15th (1). Strange!\" [1286] Crabbe's bright little eyes darted from one person to another, and they all seemed to be hiding a malicious glee, as if he were being played for a fool. Mr. Rains murmured, \"This tall, thin British gentleman came on May sixth and pulled a little trick to get your letters and envelopes. Very well, Mr. Crabbe, we're making progress. Once we settle this, we won't bother you anymore. Did he tell you his name?\" [1287] Crabbe smirked and looked impatiently at him. \"Did he tell me his name, Mr. Rains? You're so busy asking such a question. Did he tell me his name? Of course, he told me his name. Hmm, I can't remember. Oh, I've got it!\" He handed Mr. Rains a small business card, and Peachum quickly stood up, and all four of them looked at the name on the card. [1288] The card was very cheap, and in bold capital letters, the surname was printed:\n[1289] There was nothing else on the card\u2014no address, no phone number, no first name. [1290] \"Dr. Aless!\" exclaimed Peachum, eyebrows furrowed. [1291]\"Dr. Aless!\" The detective mumbled. [1292]\"Dr. Aless!\" Rowe pondered. [1293]\"Dr. Aless!\" Klaber nodded with a sideways glance. [1294]\"Dr. Aless!\" The old gentleman spoke with a peculiar tone, and everyone turned to look at him, but he kept staring at the business card. \"Good heavens, it seems impossible. Dr. Aless... Perkins, detective, Gordon,\" he suddenly asked, \"Do you know who Dr. Aless is?\" [1295]\"Not at all familiar.\" Perkins looked at him with sharp eyes. [1296] \"Never heard of him.\" The sergeant said. [1297] \"A little familiar.\" Rowe said as he thought. [1298] \"Oh, Gordon, I thought it would jolt your memories of your student days. He\u2014\"\n[1299] Klopeck made a grotesque dance movement, like a trained monkey. His gold-rimmed glasses slipped down his nose, and his smile was terrifying. \"I can tell you who Dr. Aless is.\" He said, puckering his withered lips like a playboy. [1300] \"Oh, you know?\" Reen quickly said. [1301] \"I mean, I can tell you who he really is, where he is. Everything! \" Klopp chuckled dryly. \"It's all coming back to me now.\" [1302] \"Oh, for heaven's sake,\" the sergeant gruffly said. \"Who is he?\" [1303] \"The moment I saw him at the museum, I knew who he was. Hmm, right.\" The elderly librarian cleared his throat. \"You didn't see him avoid my gaze, did you? He knew I recognized him, that rare scoundrel! I'll tell you, the man who came to see me seven weeks ago and left this calling card, claiming to be Dr. Ales, is\u2014Herman Sedlar!\" [1304] \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n[1305] (1) The day Julius Caesar was killed. [1306] Chapter 14\n[1307] The Battle of the Bookworms\n[1308] In a private dining room of a downtown restaurant, the people at the table were all sorting out their jumbled thoughts. Krapf revealed the secret with a mocking, triumphant air, leaving everyone stunned for a moment. Hamnet Sedley was the mysterious Dr. Aless! Krapf gloated, licking his lips and sending them to the door. Their last glimpse of him saw him standing in the doorway of the Saxon mansion, framed by the Ionic columns, his hands rubbing together as if the legs of a cricket. He hunched his shoulders and tilted his head, as if saying, \"Well, you think your Dr. Sedley is your Dr. Aless, don't you? Old Krapf isn't a fool, is he? He exuded a sense of victory, as if he had satisfied his violent urges on a mob after executing a private lynching. [1309] Gordon Rowe had been preoccupied the whole time and now joined the small group, sitting quietly and watching the sunlight filter through the car window onto Percy's hair, but he seemed not to really see. [1310] \"There's one very odd thing,\" Mr. Philo Vance said as they sat down around the table, \"I admit I don't understand. That wretched old man, he was certainly impressive\u2014full of dramatic elements\u2014and what he said was all true. He's the kind of man who likes to tell the truth, especially when he knows it will hurt. But... Hamnet Sedley! Of course not.\" [1311] \"If Claver says his guest was Sedley,\" the young Rowe said dejectedly, \"then you can bet your boots it was Sedley.\" [1312] \"No, Gordon,\" Percy sighed. \"Sedley couldn't have been the one who visited Claver on May 6th. The board of the London Guildhall Museum held a farewell dinner for Dr. Ales on May 7th, so it couldn't have been him. Dr. Ales visited Claver in New York on May 6th. He's not a ghost; he couldn't have crossed the Atlantic in one night.\" [1313] \"Well, it's just too eerie. I know Claver, and I'll tell you, he's not lying. Every time he tells the truth and causes a stir, he's delighted, just like Mr. Vance said.\" [1314] \"Claver was absolutely certain,\" Percy said despairingly, poking at his roast. \"He swore the man was Sedley.\" [1315] \"What's the big deal?\" Inspector Belsey glared at Rowe. \"The old devil is lying, and that's that.\" [1316] \"Well,\" Raine said, \"he could be making up a story. These old bookworms can be jealous of each other's careers. Anyway, we're not getting anywhere with this. The whole thing is too bizarre...there's something I need to tell you about Dr. Allis.\" [1317] \"Oh, yes! You were about to tell us, but Klabber interrupted...isn't that name fictitious?\" [1318] \"Of course not! That's why it's so unusual, dear. Gordon, when you were at the Saxon's, you seemed to be on the verge of remembering something. Do you remember who Dr. Allis is now?\" [1319] \"I'm sorry, sir, I thought I remembered. Probably I came across the name while doing research.\" [1320] \"It's possible. I've never met Dr. Allis myself, and I know nothing about him personally, but I do know one thing. Unless this isn't a remarkable coincidence, such a person does exist, and he's a very clever and well-educated scholar of literature.\" The old gentleman thoughtfully chewed a stalk of celery, \"A few years ago\u2014oh, eight or ten years ago, there was an article in the Sturford Quarterly\u2014a journal devoted to bibliography\u2014\"\n[1321] \"Right!\" Rowe exclaimed, \"I used to receive it regularly when I was at university.\" [1322] \"That reminds you. The point is, the author of that article was Dr. Allais.\" [1323] \"A British magazine?\" Sam asked. [1324] \"Yes. I don't remember the specifics, but Dr. Allais discussed some ridiculous and lengthy controversies about Bacon, and some of the things he said greatly offended me. So, I wrote a long article in my own name to refute him, which was published in the quarterly. Dr. Allais was also quite angry and responded in the magazine's correspondence column. We went back and forth for several issues in the quarterly.\" [1325] \"I remember,\" Rowe said eagerly, his chin jutting forward, \"It was a heated debate. That's the guy, no doubt about it!\" [1326] \"Do you know where he lives?\" the Chief suddenly asked. [1327] \"Unfortunately, no.\" [1328] \"We can look up this magazine.\" [1329] \"I'm afraid not, Chief. Mr. Roche can tell you that The Stoke Quarterly went out of print five years ago.\" [1330] \"Damn it! Alright, I'll send another telegram to Trunch, asking him to look into it. You want\u2014\"\n[1331] \"By the way, Gordon,\" said the old gentleman, \"have you had a chance to check on the binding of the Jaggard edition of the 1599 folio we discussed? Any leads on a secret related to the binding?\" [1332] Rowe shrugged. \"Nothing so far. I did trace the binding back to about a hundred and fifty years ago\u2014it's not something a person would do. The current binding is at least that old, and as for the documents inside\u2014nothing so far. No leads yet.\" [1333] \"Hmm,\" said Lanyon, his eyes flashing, and then he resumed eating his salad. [1334] Percyns pushed his plate away impatiently. \"Oh, I can't chew it. This damned case is getting on my nerves. The whole business of Dr. Siddar being the Ales is so absurd, and yet it keeps going around in my head, and I can't get rid of it. Other things are so clear\u2014\"\n[1335] \"For example\u2014\" said the inspector skeptically. [1336] \"Dr. Aless's clues. Dad, you know that the man with the rainbow mustache who came to our office on May 6th was Dr. Aless.\" [1337] \"How did you come to that conclusion?\" the young Rowe whispered. [1338] \"He went to visit the Saxons that morning, took the library's stationery from them. He must have put on that ridiculous outfit in some hotel's washroom. He wrote down the symbols - annoying symbols! - put on his strange outfit, and went to Dad's office. These things are clear.\" [1339] \"It seems possible,\" said the old gentleman. [1340] \"He didn't think he'd be - killed,\" Percy bit his lip, \"he thought no one would know his secret, his million-dollar secret. Doesn't that sound stupid?... But he was a cunning man, he wouldn't take any risks. If he had called on the 20th and was fine, nothing would have happened, the envelope would still be sealed; if he hadn't called, we would have opened the envelope, seen the Saxon stationery, tracked down Klaber, and discovered this strange Dr. Aless - he must have told Klaber that fabricated story, so Klaber would remember - by the time we went looking for him, we would have known his name and his profession\u2026\"\n[1341] \"What an awful logical analysis!\" Young Rowe smiled slightly. [1342] \"So he asked you not to open the envelope unless I was there,\" Rene said quietly. \"He knew I would remember our debate in the quarterly; so I was brought in to confirm that Dr. All\u00e8s was a book collector.\" [1343] \"He must have planned it all from the start. If things didn't go well, like now, we'd have to look for Dr. All\u00e8s, a bookworm or something. How do we go about it?\" [1344] \"It's easy,\" the inspector said carelessly. \"Petti, that's my job. He said if he didn't call, it meant he was in trouble, right? That means we know where he disappeared from, apart from his physical appearance, name, profession, or occupation - he must have been mixed up somewhere or killed.\" [1345] \"Well said, Inspector,\" Reth murmured. \"Right on the mark. You must collect all the police reports of murders, kidnappings, or disappearances from May 20th - the day he didn't call on time - to a few days ago.\" [1346] The sergeant growled, \"I know, I know. Do you know how much work that is?\" [1347] \"Not as bad as you think, Sergeant. What you're looking for is clear. Percy has already said it.\" [1348] \"Alright!\" Sam said glumly. \"I'll do it. But what's in it for me? I have to live, you know.\" [1349] Mr. J. B. Rathbone sent Sam the sergeant back to the office, dropped Miss Samantha Percy and Gordon Rowe off at Central Park, and signaled to Dromio, then sat back in the car, deep in thought. Now no one was watching him, he sat quietly in the back seat, clutching his cane handle tightly, staring at the back of Dromio's head, his face a picture of shifting emotions. Unlike most old men, he was not accustomed to talking to himself aloud, possibly because his deaf ear had not made him prone to such habits. He thought things out in pictures, some of which were so bizarre that he had to close his eyes to see them more clearly. [1350] The Lincoln limousine swiftly drove uptown, heading towards Westchester. [1351] After a long time, the old man opened his eyes and saw the lush trees and winding driveway leading to the garage. He leaned forward and lightly tapped Dromio's shoulder. [1352] \"Dromio, didn't I tell you to go to Dr. Martini's first?\" [1353] Dromio, the faithful driver, turned his head slightly so that his master could see his lips. \"Mr. Vance, is there an emergency? Are you feeling unwell again?\" [1354] The old gentleman smiled. \"No, I'm fine. This visit is purely out of scientific interest.\" [1355] \"Oh.\" Dromio scratched his left ear, shrugged, and pressed down on the accelerator. [1356] He parked the car in front of a house near Ephraim. The house was half-hidden in the trees, with vines and late-blooming roses growing outside. A silver-haired, imposing man sat on the porch smoking a pipe. [1357] \"Ah, Martini,\" Reine stretched his legs. \"Lucky you're home at this time.\" [1358] The imposing man widened his eyes. \"Mr. Reine, what brings you here? Please come in, please come in.\" [1359] Reine chuckled and closed the door behind him. \"Don't be surprised, old man. I'm feeling great.\" They shook hands, and Dr. Martini's tired eyes looked at him professionally. \"You look good, don't you?\" [1360] \"Excellent. How's your heart?\" [1361] \"It's beating merrily. I can't say the same for my stomach.\" [1362] They entered the doctor's house. A fluffy dog sniffed at Lane's ankle and then walked away indifferently. \"I don't understand, at my age\u2014\"\n[1363] \"Dear great man, a lifetime of theater menus doesn't do wonders for digestion. Sit down. I managed to sneak away from the hospital for a few hours. The hospital is boring, and I haven't come across an interesting case.\" [1364] Lane smiled. \"I have one for you.\" [1365] Dr. Martini took the pipe out of his mouth. \"Ah, I think I know. Not yourself, I presume?\" [1366] \"No, no.\" [1367] \"A troublesome matter?\" Martini said with a half-smile. \"I'll give up the afternoon countryside fun.\" [1368] \"No need.\" The old man leaned forward. \"This case \u2013 I believe \u2013 can be diagnosed while sitting in a chair.\" He suddenly looked around. \"Martini, I think you should close the door.\" [1369] The doctor was puzzled and then stood up, shutting the sunlight outside. [1370] \"You're mysterious,\" he said, sitting back in the chair with the pipe in his mouth. \"Confidential? I guess it's a criminal case, but there's no one here to listen \u2013\"\n[1371] Laine's gaze was stern. \"Martini, when a person goes deaf, they feel like the walls have ears. Old friend, I've gotten involved in this most incredible event, with many branches at every point\u2026\"\n[1372] Dromio, dozing at the wheel, swatted a bee that had landed on his collar, somewhat surprised. The sweet fragrance of roses intoxicated him. Martini's door had been closed for half an hour and now opened, revealing his tall figure. Dromio heard Martini say lightly, \"It's probably the only way, Mr. Laine. I must see the paper before I can give you an opinion. But even so, as I said\u2026\"\n[1373] \"You scientists!\" Dromio heard Laine's tone of impatience. \"I used to hope the problem would be clearer. But\u2026\" He shrugged and extended his hand. \"Thank you for showing interest. I think I deserve some credit. I'll bring the paper to you tonight.\" [1374] \"Ah, yes, I'll come to Hamlet's Manor tonight.\" [1375] \"Oh, nonsense! That would be too much trouble for you. I'll come here again\u2026\"\n[1376] \"Don't say that. Driving does me good, and I want to see old Quincannon. The last time I saw him, his arteries were in a bad state.\" [1377] Dromio didn't understand what was going on and opened the door. His master quickly walked down the path, stopped, and raised his eyebrows sharply, saying sharply, \"Did you see anyone lurking around here?\" [1378] Dromio was startled. \"Lurking, sir? Philo?\" [1379] \"Yes, did you see anyone else?\" [1380] Dromio scratched his ear. \"I probably dozed off for a minute or two, sir, but I don't think\u2026\"\n[1381] \"Ah, Dromio Jr.,\" the old gentleman sighed, climbing into the car, \"when will you learn to be more alert... I suppose it doesn't matter. He waved cheerfully to Dr. Martini, \"Pull over at the gas station, Dromio Jr., to the telegraph office.\" [1382] The car drove away. When they reached the gas station, Dromio Jr. found the telegraph office, and Philo Roen walked in. He glanced at the clock on the wall, then sat down at a small table, reaching for a stack of yellow paper and a chained pencil. For a few seconds, he stared at the pencil tip. The pencil was sharp, but he didn't notice. His eyes were focused on something else.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nSo the second person must have been the victim; he didn't leave the house because Maxwell didn't hear the doorbell warning again, and all the doors and windows were locked from the inside. But if he was the victim, and he was alone in the house, who killed him? No, like Gordon said, the one who left the house was the murderer, and the murderer was the masked man.\" [2430] \"What does this lead to?\" Lane asked slowly. [2431] \"The murderer!\" [2432] \"Yes!\" Rowe agreed. [2433] \"I'll tell you\u2014don't talk, Gordon! There were two people in the house that night. One of them was the victim, one of the Seldrake brothers\u2014the dead man couldn't be made up like that. Well, one of them walked into the house and knew exactly where the files were\u2014he went straight to the secret cabinet in the study. The other didn't know, and he almost wrecked the whole house looking for it. Well, who's most likely to know where the hiding place is?\" [2434] \"That Alec fellow\u2014William Seldrake,\" the Inspector answered. [2435] \"Right, Dad. Because the hiding place was set up by him, and the stuff was stowed away by him. Since the second visitor knew the hiding place\u2014the first one, the axeman, didn't\u2014so Alec is the second person. The fact that the second person easily entered the house confirms this. The door always closes automatically, Maxwell's duplicate key was taken by the first person, but the second person could still get in, using Alec's original key, isn't it?\" [2436] \"Who do you think the masked man was?\" her father asked. [2437] \"There's evidence for that too. We found fragments of a single lens in the hallway. Dr. Sedra is the only person involved in the case who wears a single lens. Maxwell never saw a single lens in the house. This means that Hamnett Sedra was in the house on the night of the murder! If Hamnett was in the house, then he's one of the two, the other being his brother William - Dr. Alec. But since William was the victim, as I said, Hamnett must have been the one who killed his own brother!\" [2438] \"Damn!\" Sam said. [2439] \"No, no, Percy,\" Roarke jumped up, \"it's -\"\n[2440] \"Wait, Gordon,\" Reine quietly said, \"Percy, what makes you think Dr. Hamnett Sedra is the main character in this case?\" [2441] Percy glared angrily at Rowe. \"I have several reasons why Dr. Hamnett Sedgwick would want to get his hands on Shakespeare's manuscript. First, he is a bibliophile, and he admits that William told him everything about the manuscript. I dare say he is a typical bookworm and would not let the opportunity to see Shakespeare's true work slip away. Another suspicious point is that he suddenly gave up his position as the curator of the London Kingston Museum and accepted a similar position in the United States, which he hated, and with a lower salary - coincidentally, this allowed him to conveniently contact the J\u00e4ger of Saxony! Finally, he secretly arrived in New York before the scheduled date.\" [2442] Reine sighed: \"Percy, you are truly brilliant.\" [2443] \"And -\" Percy continued enthusiastically, \"Hamnett being the axeman is based on the fact that only he does not know where the manuscript is hidden, so he must slash blindly, like the real act of the one with the axe... It is easy to recreate the scene with two Sedgwick in the house. Hamnett was slashing blindly in William's bedroom upstairs when William came in, took the things from the hidden place in the study. They met soon after, and Hamnett saw the document in William's hand, so he swung the axe at his watch and wrist. During the struggle, Hamnett's single lens fell and broke. Hamnett shot William, dragged the body into -\"\n[2444] \"No!\" Rowe shouted, \"Percy, shut up. Mr. Reine, listen to me. Everything I just said can be agreed upon to some extent - William and Hamnett are the two people in the house, William is the one who took the document, and Hamnett is the masked man and the axeman. But it was not William who was killed by Hamnett, but Hamnett who was killed by William! The corpse in the rubble could be either of them. I believe that the person who called himself Hamnett and was almost starving when we found him in the house is actually William!\" [2445] \"Gordon,\" Percy countered, \"that - that is stupid. You forgot that the original key to the house was found on the corpse, which proves that the corpse is William's.\" [2446] \"Ah, no, Perkins,\" Lane interjected, \"that doesn't make sense logically. Gordon, go on. Why do you think this clever argument is wrong?\" [2447] \"Based on psychology, sir. I admit it's hard to find evidence to support my point of view. I believe the person lying in the hospital bed lied about his identity because, as William Sedley, he was a wanted fugitive by the French police; as a survivor, he now has the documents and wants to dispose of them freely. Don't forget that he knows all about it and can say things that are in his favor. The inspector and the reporter talked the night before, and all the details were published in the newspaper, and the rest he could find out the next morning from the reporter.\" [2448] Lane chuckled oddly. \"Gordon, I agree theoretically that this motive could be established, and this argument is very clever. But who placed the bomb?\" [2449] Perkins and Rowe exchanged glances. Then both hastily agreed that the bomb was placed by a third party twenty-four hours before the murder, with the sole purpose of destroying the documents, for reasons unknown. After placing the bomb, the third party thought his work was done and disappeared from the scene. [2450] The elderly gentleman grumbled. \"But what about the kidnapping? Why did the survivor \u2013 whether it was William or Hamnet \u2013 deliberately get involved in this mess and then be helplessly discovered by the police? Do you remember how exhausted and hungry he was when we found him?\" [2451] \"That's easy,\" Percy retorted, \"whether it's William or Hamnett, their motives are the same: to frame the deceased for the fake kidnapping, thus strengthening the apparent innocence of the conspirator.\" [2452] Rowe nodded, somewhat doubtfully. [2453] \"What about Donohue?\" asked the inspector. [2454] \"If Hamnett is the survivor,\" answered Percy, \"then he is the one who kidnapped Donohue because Donohue saw him leaving the Alley's house and thought he was a co-conspirator with William. He may have thought that by kidnapping Donohue, he could get the location of the hidden documents from him\u2014remember he threatened to torture him?\" [2455] \"But if William is the survivor\u2026\" Rowe pointed out sharply, \"then it was he who kidnapped Donohue because Donohue was following him and might have threatened his plan.\" [2456] \"So now the question is,\" Reen continued, \"you both agree that Hamnet Sedley and William Sedley are involved in this crime, but you disagree on who killed whom. I must say, that's quite a predicament!\" [2457] \"Good heavens!\" the sergeant suddenly exclaimed, his eyes bulging, \"this couldn't have come at a worse time!\" [2458] \"Dad, what do you mean?\" Petty asked. [2459] \"Percy, before you came back, Reen told us that he believes this British man may have been lying about his identity, and we have a way to test if he's telling the truth!\" [2460] \"A way to test?\" Percy furrowed his brow, \"I can't think of one.\" [2461] \"It's actually quite simple,\" Rene said as he stood up. \"We need to go to Britain. Gordon, did you leave the man who claims to be Hamnet Sedley there?\" [2462] \"Yes, sir.\" [2463] \"Excellent. Let's go. This should only take five minutes to resolve.\" [2464] Chapter 29\n[2465] Visual Illusion\n[2466] They found the person who called himself Hamnet Sedley working with Dr. Jott in the library director's office. The director was somewhat surprised when they walked in, but the British man quickly stood up and smiled over to them. His pleasant demeanor faded as he saw their serious expressions. [2467] \"It's a pleasure to see you,\" he said in a friendly tone, but the smile disappeared when he saw their stern faces. \"I hope there's no trouble.\" [2468] \"We all hope so.\" The sergeant said tersely, \"Dr. Jott, would you mind if we speak with Dr. Sedley privately? It's a matter of great importance.\" [2469] \"Importance?\" The director had risen from his seat and stood still, looking at each of them before lowering his head and rummaging through some papers. \"Oh\u2014of course.\" A blush slowly rose on his face. He moved around the desk and quickly left the room. Dr. Sedley remained still, and there was silence in the room for a moment. Then Sam nodded to Raine, who took a step forward. [2470] \"Dr. Sedley,\" Raine's face showed no emotion, \"For...let's say, for scientific interest, we must conduct a very simple test on you...Percy, lend me your briefcase for a moment.\" [2471] \"A test?\" The Englishman's face showed displeasure, and he put his hand in his coat pocket. [2472] Bishop quickly handed the handbag to Rain. He opened the handbag, looked inside, took out a brightly colored handkerchief, and then closed the handbag. \"Very well, sir, please tell me, what color is this handkerchief?\" [2473] Bishop held his breath, his eyes widening with some realization, while the others stared blankly at the scene. [2474] Dr. Sedley blushed, a complex expression surging on his hawk-like face. He took a step back and said sternly, \"This is ridiculous. What is the purpose of this childish game?\" [2475] Rain whispered, \"Of course, just to confirm the innocent color of this little handkerchief, no harm, right?\" [2476] There was a silence. Then the British man spoke without turning around, his tone stiff, \"Blue.\" - The handkerchief had green, yellow, and white colors. [2477] \"Dr. Sedra's tie, Reine?\" Reine continued to ask, his expression unchanged. [2478] The British man swayed, his eyes pained. \"Brown.\" - It was actually sapphire blue. [2479] \"Thank you.\" Reine returned the handkerchief and briefcase to Perkins. \"Inspector, this man is not Dr. Hamnet Sedra, he is William Sedra, sometimes called Dr. Alessi.\" [2480] The British man suddenly collapsed into a chair, burying his face in his hands. [2481] \"God, how did you know?\" Sam whispered. [2482] Raine sighed. \"Inspector, this is basic work. On May 6th, Dr. Alessi, or William Sedra, went to your office and entrusted you with that envelope. That person could not be Dr. Hamnett Sedra, as he himself said. Dr. Hamnett Sedra was in London on May 7th for a reception in his honor. The man with the envelope, of course, was the one who wrote the symbols inside - he admitted as much in your office that morning. What does the paper and symbols represent?\" [2483] \"Ah, just... damn, I don't know.\" The inspector said. [2484] Raine tiredly said: \"The paper was a light gray, with the heading 'Saxony Library' in a deeper gray. This and the form of the symbols immediately reminded me.\" [2485] \"What does it mean? We just made a mistake. You were lucky, you just guessed right.\" [2486] \"That's right. In other words, William Sedra wrote Wm SHe upside down on the paper. To correctly read those symbols, one must turn the paper upside down, which is very unusual. Someone picks up a paper with a heading, intending to write on it, and instinctively puts the paper right side up \u2013 that is, with the name and address at the top. However, the person who wrote these symbols took the paper and wrote it upside down! Why?\" [2487] \"I see.\" Percy sighed. \"Unless it was an accident, he couldn't see the printed markings!\" [2488] \"Yes, dear, that's exactly it. It seems impossible, but it's more likely that Dr. Aless accidentally turned the paper over and wrote a few letters, not realizing it would make any difference to the person who saw the symbols later. However, another possibility is logically possible, and I cannot ignore it. I asked myself: if it were true, what kind of miracle could this phenomenon create? Why didn't Dr. Aless see the deep gray print on the Saxon paper? Was he blind? But that's hard to believe. The person who came to your office seemed to have good eyesight. Then I remembered something else, and suddenly saw the answer... the mustache.\" [2489] The British man lifted his pain-filled eyes, now filled with curiosity. He murmured, \"Mustache?\" [2490] \"You know,\" Reine smiled, \"he still doesn't understand what's wrong with the fake mustache he's wearing! Mr. Sedra, the mustache you wore that day was terrifying! It was a shocking blue-green, and who knows what other colors!\" [2491] Sedgwick opened his mouth wide, groaning, \"Oh, I bought it at a costume shop. I guess I didn't explain it well enough. The shopkeeper thought I wanted a... a ridiculous mustache for some crazy thing like dressing up as a ghost...\u201d\n[2492] \"Unfortunately,\" Renault said coldly, \"but the mustache and the note match. I think the person who wrote the symbol is very likely to be completely colorblind. I've heard of such cases, so I asked Dr. Martini. He told me that complete colorblindness is extremely rare. But when it happens, such a person sees all colors as different shades of gray, like a pencil sketch. He also mentioned another possibility - that he may not be completely colorblind, but has no color perception. That's better than being colorblind, but he can't see the differences in brightness of printed colors. When Dr. Martini checked the Saxon library note, he was quite sure that the person who wrote the symbol was affected by this visual defect.\" [2493] The British man moved, growled, \"I've never seen a color.\" [2494] They were silent for a moment. Renault sighed and continued, \"So I believe in my heart that Dr. Alessi is colorblind. Sir, you were just tormented by the same situation. You guessed the colors of Miss Sam's handkerchief and Mr. Rowe's tie, not knowing the real colors. Now you call yourself Hamnett Sedgwick, but Hamnett Sedgwick is not colorblind! We first saw him in this museum's Saxon room. He checked the repaired cabinet - the one with the stolen 1599 Jagger - and correctly identified the colors of the book covers in it, as well as the different shades of the same color. He could distinguish that a book cover was golden brown, which a colorblind person could not distinguish such subtle differences. Since you are either William or Hamnett, and Hamnett's vision is normal, while William is colorblind - you are colorblind, obviously you are William, and this reasoning is very simple. I proposed this test to see if you were lying. You did lie. Most of the story you told us in the hospital was fabricated, although I suspect there were some truths. Now please tell the whole story to my friend.\" [2495] He sat down in a chair and wiped his lips again. [2496] \"Okay,\" the British man's voice was low, \"I am William Zedra.\" [2497] When he first visited the inspector, he was Dr. Aless. He gave the letter with the symbol to the inspector; in case something happened to him while he was searching for Shakespeare's documents, it could serve as a clue - at the time, he thought the likelihood of something happening was very small. On June 20th, he didn't call because he couldn't, the very small possibility had occurred. His brother Hamnett - William found out then - accepted the position of director of the British Museum, with the sole purpose of approaching the 1599 Jagger in the Saxon collection. William stole the Jagger from the museum the night he did, and then Hamnett kidnapped William, which happened shortly after Donohue appeared, on the same night Donohue was also kidnapped by Hamnett. The Irishman had been unconscious for a while, so his sense of time was distorted... from William stealing the Jagger to the police rescuing him from the abandoned house, he was a prisoner. [2498] No matter how Hamnett threatened him, William refused to reveal the location of the documents. Of course, Donohue didn't know anything, and couldn't tell Hamnett anything. Hamnett, in order to go to the museum to work and pretend to be innocent, came and went to the place of confinement in a hurry, and couldn't do it regularly, so he became desperate. One day, he told William that he knew the documents were in William's house, and he put a bomb in the cellar to blow up the house and the documents! The bomb was made by a black market chemist on his own. It was then that William realized that his brother's true intention was not to preserve the Shakespeare documents, but to destroy them! [2499] \"But why?\" Rowe raised his fist and roared, \"That's... that's the most barbaric act of destruction! Oh, why destroy it?\" [2500] \"Is your brother insane?\" Percy exclaimed. [2501] The British man clenched his lips, looking at Reine, who was calmly looking into the distance. \"I don't know,\" he said. [2502] Hamnet set the timer on the bomb for twenty-four hours later. William realized that if he let the bomb go off, the documents would be destroyed and forever lost. He had to give up resistance, knowing that any delay would be more advantageous. He might be able to untie himself and save the documents. So he told Hamnet the location and method of opening the secret cabinet, but he couldn't escape. Hamnet proudly told him that he would go back to William's house and destroy the documents himself, and that he had plenty of time. He would remove the bomb's safety pin\u2026 Hamnet took William's key\u2014the original one\u2014and William never saw him alive again.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nWe're dealing with an honest, even conscientious thief - something that has never been seen in the history of theft. The true Villon (1)! And more?\" [955] \"I think things are clear,\" the young man suddenly said. \"He returned the original fake Jagger without cutting open the cover, which means he is very familiar with rare books. I can tell you, I've seen the fake book, and it's not bad at all, something that most people wouldn't notice. He checked the book and immediately found that it was not real. Since he was only looking for the genuine 1599 Jagger, he didn't even touch the fake one and returned it.\" [956] \"So, he's probably a collector or something like that, right?\" Percy whispered. [957] \"Yes, dear, that's a very good analysis,\" the old man said, getting up and pacing around the room. \"We've put together a very enlightening picture. A scholar, an antiquarian, a collector, fundamentally honest, who went to great lengths to commit theft in order to obtain\u2014I'm sure\u2014a paper hidden inside an old and valuable book. Interesting, isn't it?\" [958] Sam interjected, \"I wonder what he could gain from it?\" [959] \"The opening, or the recess,\" Roe thought for a moment, \"is about five inches long and three inches wide. If it's a piece of paper, it might be folded and could be very old.\" [960] \"I think so,\" Raine murmured. \"Although perhaps not. Well, things are quite clear now. I think I should do some small investigations of my own.\" [961] \"About Donoho?\" The sergeant asked hopefully. [962] Lain smiled. \"No. That's your job; you'll do it better than I would. I'm thinking of...,\" he continued, frowning, \"a little research. You know, I have a rather remarkable library of my own.\" [963] \"It's a scholar's paradise,\" Roe said enviously. [964] \"What kind of research?\" Pechinsk asked. [965] \"Dear, check if the damaged Jagger's leather binding is the original. If that doesn't help, it can at least clarify some things. The leather binding of this book could provide clues to determine the year the hidden item was placed there. As Gordon said, it could be some folded document.\" [966] \"Mr. Raine, perhaps I can assist,\" the young man said eagerly. [967] \"Ah!\" The old gentleman said, \"Gordon, that's a good idea. You can work independently, and we can compare our findings.\" [968] \"I agree,\" Percy said, inexplicably happy, \"If there's a document hidden in such an old book, it could be a record. But how did the thief know, and where to look?\" [969] \"Astute observations! I have a similar thought. I'll look through all the information about the first edition of 'The Devout Pilgrim' from 1599. It may provide some precise dates. Jagger was involved in the distribution of many publications in Elizabethan London, and his name is closely associated with the literary world. Good, good, this is certainly a logical step. Gordon, what do you think?\" [970] \"I'll look into it too,\" Rowe said quietly. [971] \"Excellent. Inspector, will you continue to pursue Donohue?\" [972] \"As much as I can. I'll have Detective Sergeant Grayson do most of the work.\" [973] \"Indeed, that's his job. Inspector, I don't see any financial gain in this case.\" [974] \"You're right,\" the inspector said loudly, \"but it annoyed me, so I'll indulge myself.\" [975] \"Still as stubborn as ever,\" the old man chuckled. \"I have a suggestion for you, if you're investigating this case out of curiosity. Why not investigate Dr. Hamnett Sedra?\" [976] The sergeant was startled. Pechins stopped lighting a cigarette for Rowe. \"That duke? Why?\" [977] \"Let's say it's intuition,\" Rene murmured. \"Did you notice the strange look our friend Klaber gave Dr. Siddartha?\" [978] \"Good heavens, yes!\" exclaimed Percy, \"Gordon, you noticed it too.\" [979] \"You call him Gordon?\" the officer muttered suspiciously. [980] \"It was purely accidental.\" Mr. Lawrie hastened to say, \"Miss Sam was too excited. Miss Sam, please call me Mr. Lawrie... Yes, Patty, I noticed that, I've been trying to make sense of it all.\" [981] \"What's all this about Gordon, Petty?\" the sergeant growled. \"So familiar.\" [982] \"All right, all right, sergeant,\" J. R. Rine interceded. \"Don't let your personal feelings get in the way of the discussion. You know you're an old fossil. Young people aren't like they used to be.\" [983] \"Dad,\" Peasmarsh blushed. [984] \"Not like your time,\" Roarke added. [985] \"Introductions, flirting, kissing in dark corners,\" Rine continued with a smile. \"Well, sergeant, you have to accept these things. I said Clurman was a slippery character, trying to cover his tracks, but there are some strange things going on that need looking into.\" [986] \"Well, anyway,\" the sergeant grumbled, \"I still don't like... huh? I didn't think of that. If so, I think we should ask Klabber a few questions.\" [987] Pencils examined the cigarette butt, his voice low: \"Dad, you know, this makes me think. Why don't we investigate Dr. Sedlar first instead of bothering Mr. Klabber?\" [988] \"Patti, you mean Britain?\" [989] \"Let's not be greedy. How about the shipping company?\" [990] \"Shipping company? What's that supposed to mean?\" [991] \"Just to see if there's any drama,\" Peachins murmured. [992] Forty-five minutes later, the sergeant put down the phone, wiping his brow with a trembling handkerchief. He finally sighed: \"Well, the show's about to start. It's too absurd... You know what the shipping company's manager just told me?\" [993] \"Oh, Dad,\" Peachins said, \"you're teasing us. What did he say?\" [994] \"There's no Hamnet Sedra on the passenger list!\" [995] They all looked at each other in surprise. Then Gordon Rowe whistled, putting out his cigarette in the sergeant's ashtray. \"So that's it,\" he murmured. \"The famous Dr. Sedra...\"\n[996] \"Good, I like it,\" Percy murmured. \"I like it a lot.\" [997] \"Good heavens, he's a fake!\" Sam exclaimed, \"Listen, kid, you can't tell a soul. Not a word, or else I'll\u2014\"\n[998] \"Alright, alright, sergeant,\" Reine interrupted him gently, leaning back on the leather sofa with many wrinkles on his forehead, \"Don't be in such a hurry. A good set-up doesn't necessarily make a good play, and a suspicious situation can't convict someone. Why did you describe Sedra to the secretary?\" [999] \"Well,\" Sam snorted, \"he checked the passenger list and couldn't find the guy's name. I described Sedra to the secretary and asked him to check with the ship's staff. The ship docked this morning, and they're all still on duty, so he did it right away. Well, Sedra not only isn't on the passenger list, but no one remembers anyone on board who looks like Sedra!\" [1000] \"Interesting,\" Rowe pondered. [1001] \"I admit the strange smell has grown stronger,\" muttered the old gentleman, \"strange, very strange\u2026\"\n[1002] \"Can't you see what this means?\" Percy exclaimed, \"It means that Sedra has been in the country at least four days!\" [1003] \"How did you figure that out, Percy?\" his father asked. [1004] \"He didn't fly across the Atlantic, did he? You remember I called the shipping company last Thursday to ask when the next ship from Britain was due in port - Sally Bostwick wrote to me saying she was coming, but she didn't say when. They said there was a ship on Saturday, the next one was today, and there were no other ships due in port. Since today is Tuesday, I figured this British fellow must have been in New York at least four days - at least since last Saturday.\" [1005] \"Even longer,\" Rowe frowned, \"Sedra! Unbelievable!\" [1006] \"You might check the Saturday ship,\" Raine said casually. [1007] The sergeant reached for the phone, then sat back down. \"I have a better idea, kill two birds with one stone,\" he pressed a button, and Miss Brody appeared in the office as if by magic, \"Did you bring your notebook? Good, send a telegram to Scotland Yard!\" [1008] \"To \u2013 to whom, Sergeant?\" Miss Brody stammered, surprised to see the tall, strong young man at the door. [1009] \"Scotland Yard. I want this slippery fellow to see how we do things here!\" The sergeant's face turned red, \"Don't you know where Scotland Yard is? London, England!\" [1010] \"Yes \u2013 yes, sir,\" Miss Brody hastily replied. [1011] \"Mark it for Inspector Trench, T-r-e-n-c-h. 'Request all information on the former director of the London Golden Museum, Hamnet Sedlar, now in New York. Include departure date from Britain, physical description, social connections, reputation, and any other available information. Keep confidential. Best regards. Send immediately.'\" [1012] Miss Brawdy stumbled towards Rowley. [1013] \"Wait, how did you spell Sedlar's name?\" [1014] \"S-e-d-d-l-e-r,\" Miss Brawdy stammered, her face pale with excitement. [1015] The inspector took a deep breath and smiled. \"Good, good, Miss Brawdy,\" he said calmly, \"Don't faint. It's all right. Good heavens, can't you even spell? It's S-e-d-l-a-r!\" [1016] \"Oh, yes, sir.\" Miss Broude quickly ran away. [1017] \"Poor Miss Broude,\" Pecchini chuckled. \"Dad, you always scare her like that. Maybe it's because of the strange young man around... Oh, Mr. Lane, what's wrong?\" She spoke with caution. [1018] Lane looked surprised and stared at Sam as if he had never seen him before, or as if he wasn't looking at him, and then jumped up. [1019] \"Good heavens!\" he exclaimed. \"So that's it!\" He paced around the room, muttering to himself. [1020] \"What's going on?\" the inspector asked, surprised. [1021] \"This name! This name! Hamnet Sedley... Good heavens, it's... it's... unbelievable! If it's a coincidence, then there's no justice in the world.\" [1022] \"Name?\" Percy frowned. \"What's so strange about a name, Mr. Lane? It sounds like proper English to me.\" [1023] Gordon Rowe's mouth hung open, like a digger, and the mischievous glint in his brown eyes was replaced by a look of astonishment and comprehension. [1024] Mr. Lane stopped pacing, rubbing his chin, and burst out in a series of low chuckles. \"Of course, it's proper English, Percy. You have a knack for getting to the heart of the matter. You're absolutely right. Not only English, but historical English. Ha! Gordon, I see your eyes shining. He stopped laughing, suddenly sitting down, and spoke in a serious tone, \"I knew I had heard that name before.\" [1025] The inspector looked blank. \"I haven't heard of any trouble.\" [1026] \"Well, Percy, you're more educated than your father. You've studied British literature, haven't you?\" [1027] \"Of course.\" [1028] \"Have you studied Elizabethan literature?\" [1029] Percy's cheeks flushed. \"That\u2014that was a long time ago.\" [1030] The old gentleman shook his head dejectedly. \"Typical modern education. So you've never heard of Hamnet Sedley. Gordon, tell them who Hamnet Sedley is!\" [1031] \"Hamnet Sedley\u2014\" Mr. Rowe's voice was a bit stunned, \"one of William Shakespeare's closest friends.\" [1032] \"Shakespeare!\" Sam exclaimed, \"Rayne, are you mistaken? Are you all crazy? What does old Shakespeare have to do with this?\" [1033] \"It has a lot to do with it. I began to think\u2026\" Rayne whispered, \"Right, Gordon, yes. It's just\u2026\" He pondered, shaking his head, \"You must know. Sedley\u2026 Oh my!\" [1034] \"I'm still not quite getting it,\" Percy complained. \"In that regard, I agree with my father. Of course\u2026\"\n[1035] \"This Sedley guy must be a wandering Jew, right?\" the sergeant joked. \"He can't be over three hundred years old, can he?\" He laughed merrily. [1036] \"Ha, ha!\" Mr. Rawlins sighed deeply. [1037] \"I don't mean our friend is Ahasuerus (2),\" Mr. Raine smiled, \"So far, nothing has been so exaggerated. But what I mean is, Dr. Hamnet Sadler - former director of the Kingston Museum in London, future director of the British Museum in New York, English, cultured, a book collector... Oh, Dr. Sadler is a direct descendant of Shakespeare's friend, it's not completely impossible.\" [1038] \"The family from Stratford?\" Percyns pondered. [1039] The old man shrugged, \"We know nothing about them.\" [1040] \"I think -\" Rawlins said slowly, \"The Sadler family comes from Gloucestershire.\" [1041] \"What difference does that make?\" Pecham protested. \"If Dr. Sedgwick is the descendant of Shakespeare's close friend, does the Sedgwick family have anything to do with this copy of the Jagger edition of 'The Passionate Pilgrim' from 1599, which caused all this commotion?\" [1042] Mr. Reen said quietly: \"Dear, the problem is here. Inspector, you sent a telegram to your British friends at Scotland Yard, and it gave us such inspiration. Maybe we can find out... who knows? 'The Passionate Pilgrim' itself is impossible... but that... \" He fell silent again. [1043] The inspector sat helplessly, looking at his friends and then at his daughter. Young Gordon Rowe stared at Reen, and Pecham stared at Rowe. [1044] Reen suddenly stood up and reached for his cane. They watched him silently. [1045] \"Strange,\" he said. \"Very strange.\" He nodded, lost in thought, and left the inspector's office. [1046] --- --- --- --- ---\n[1047] (1) Fran\u00e7ois Villon (1431-1463), a French poet known for his lyrical works, was often involved in drunken brawls, fights, and theft, committing numerous crimes. [1048] (2) Ahasuerus, a Persian king mentioned in the Bible. [1049] Chapter 11\n[1050] 3HS wM\n[1051]Dromio cheerfully muttered curses at the traffic policeman as he drove the black Lincoln away from Fifth Avenue. He skillfully maneuvered the car through the maze of vehicles, pulling up to a red light on Sixth Avenue. [1052]Mr. Jury sat silently in the back seat, tapping the sharp edge of a yellow sheet of paper against his lips. He had read the words written on the paper over a dozen times, his brow furrowed. It was a telegram, dated \"June 21 - 12:06 AM.\" The telegram had been delivered to Hamlet House in Westchester at dawn. [1053]\"It's strange for Sam to wire me at this hour,\" the old man thought to himself. \"In the middle of the night! It couldn't be... an emergency? Not likely...\"\n[1054]Dromio leaned over to honk the horn. A car and another were locked in a tangle at the corner, refusing to yield, with a line of cars behind them, the situation truly dismal. Jury looked ahead and saw the chaos spread to Fifth Avenue, so he leaned forward and tapped Dromio's ear. [1055]\"I think I'll walk the rest of the way,\" he said. \"Just one block. Meet me at Sam's office.\" [1056] He got out of the car, still holding the telegram, and carefully placed it in his coat pocket before walking towards Broadway. [1057] He found Sam Detective Agency in disarray. Miss Broadly sat in the reception area, her tense expression mirroring the chaos around her, her eyes sorrowfully fixed on Percy. Percy paced behind the railing, looking like an enraged sergeant, biting his lip and urgently glancing at the clock on the wall. [1058] At the sound of the door opening, she jumped up, Miss Broadly letting out a soft shriek. [1059] \"You've finally arrived!\" Percy exclaimed, gripping the old gentleman's arm tightly. \"I thought you'd never come. You're a lifesaver!\" [1060] Lean was startled as Percy embraced him, his arms wrapping around his neck, and passionately kissed his cheek. \"Dear child,\" Lean protested, \"you're trembling! What's happened? The telegram from the commissioner was so cryptic, it didn't tell me anything. Is he alright?\" [1061] \"He's doing wonderfully,\" Percy sneered, her eyes brightening as she touched the earrings on her ear, \"Now, let's get to the body!\" She pushed open the door to the chief's office, revealing an elderly, haggard gentleman with red-rimmed eyes and a pale complexion, stiffly sitting in a swivel chair, staring determinedly at the items on the desk. [1062] \"Hooray!\" he exclaimed, staggering to his feet, \"Good Lord! Pet, I told you you could count on the old coot! Sit down, Mr. Reen, sit down. It's so good to see you.\" [1063] Mr. Reen sank into the leather sofa. \"Goodness, what a welcome! I feel like a prodigal son. All right, tell me what's going on. I'm about to burst with curiosity.\" [1064] Sam picked up his meticulously studied item. \"See this?\" [1065] \"Well, my eyesight is perfectly fine, so I saw it.\" [1066] The inspector grinned. \"Alright, we're going to open it.\" [1067] Rayne looked from Sam to Peachins. \"But \u2014 alright, do as you please. Inspector, is that why you called me here?\" [1068] \"We sent you a telegram,\" Peachins blurted out.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2140] \"There's someone in the hall who wants to see you. He called as soon as you left. He seemed very upset, Mr. Jules.\" [2141] \"Who is it?\" [2142] \"He said his name is Jot.\" [2143] They hurried towards the hall. The entire manor was designed to resemble a medieval British castle, and the layout of the hall echoed the past. Standing at a distance, with his hands behind his back, was the director of the British Museum, Dr. Jot, pacing under the tragic mask that Rowe had carefully placed at one end of the hall. [2144] The three of them rushed towards him. [2145] \"Dr. Jot,\" Rowe said slowly, \"I apologize for keeping you waiting. There were some unexpected events... Your face looks as grim as that mask. What's the matter?\" [2146] \"Unexpected events?\" Dr. Jotter said angrily. \"So you know about it?\" He nodded reluctantly at Percy and Rowe. [2147] \"The explosion?\" [2148] \"Explosion? What explosion? Oh, no! I mean Dr. Sedra.\" [2149] \"Dr. Sedra!\" The three of them exclaimed. [2150] \"He's missing.\" [2151] The librarian leans against an oak table, with bloodshot eyes. [2152] \"Missing?\" Percy furrows his brow, \"How could that be? We saw him on Saturday, didn't we, Gordon?\" [2153] \"Yes, indeed.\" The librarian rumbles, \"He came in on Saturday morning and stayed for a few minutes, seemed fine. Before he left, I asked him to call me at home on Sunday \u2013 last night \u2013 to discuss some museum matters. He agreed and left.\" [2154] \"He didn't call?\" Raine whispers. [2155] \"No. I contacted the Seneca Inn where he was staying, and he wasn't there. I waited for him all day today, hoping he'd get in touch, but nothing. Dr. Jotter shrugs, \"It's \u2013 it's stupid! He didn't say he was leaving. I thought maybe he was sick, so I called the inn again this afternoon. They said they haven't seen him since Saturday morning.\" [2156] \"That doesn't mean he disappeared Saturday!\" Rowe countered. [2157] \"I know. It's just strange. I don't know what to do. Should I call the police or\u2026 I tried to reach your father, Miss Samuels, but the office secretary said\u2026\"\n[2158] \"First Donoho, then Dr. Aless, and now Sedra,\" Percy lamented. \"These people are disappearing one by one! It's \u2013 it's unacceptable!\" [2159] \"Unless Sedra is Aless,\" Rowe said. [2160] Dr. Jotter ran his fingers through his hair. \"Oh, my God!\" [2161] Pinchot frowned. \"I don't think that necessarily means Dr. Aless is Sedra, or that he escaped with the file.\" [2162] \"Dear Miss Sampson, the hotel staff said all of his things were still in the room. I don't think a person running away would do that. What file are you talking about\u2014\"\n[2163] Raine looked exhausted, with deep circles under his eyes and skin that looked like crumpled parchment. He wearily shook his head: \"These conjectures won't get us anywhere. It's a surprising turn of events\u2026 The only thing I can suggest is to find out what happened to Sedra.\" [2164] Pinchot and Roebling arrived in town late at night. They parked their car outside the Seneca Hotel and went inside to find the manager. After some hesitation, they were granted permission to look at Dr. Sedra's room. The room seemed tidy, with British-cut clothes neatly hanging in the closet, freshly laundered sheets and pillowcases in the dresser, and three unopened suitcases and bags. The manager seemed reluctant to involve the police, glancing at Pinchot's credentials\u2014which, of course, were the detective's\u2014and reluctantly allowing the search of the room. [2165] The luggage and clothes were all British. There were some letters in the room, with postmarks from \"London\" and addressed to Dr. Hamnett Sedra, apparently from former British colleagues. The passport and visa were in order, issued to Dr. Hamnett Sedra, with a familiar-looking photo attached. [2166] \"It's Sedra, no doubt,\" Rowe said grimly. \"I'm starting to get paranoid about this. There's no sign here that this person wanted to flee the country.\" [2167] \"Ugh!\" Peachins muttered under his breath. \"Gordon, take me home, and\u2014and, kiss me!\" [2168] Chapter 25\n[2169] Murder\n[2170] The sun shone brightly. The fire had been extinguished, and the smoke had dispersed with the night, leaving only burnt-out ashes, piles of rubble, and charred trees to bear witness to the explosion of the night before. Firefighters and police were busy digging through the rubble. A dark-skinned man calmly directed the entire operation, his eyes sharp and eager to clear away the debris so he could descend into the cellar to see what was inside. [2171] They stood by the edge of the woods, the warm morning breeze r