] \"The second visitor \u2013 the one who took the documents out of the secret cabinet \u2013\" Perkins looked pointedly at Reen, \"could not have been the one who planted the bomb. It's obvious. The second visitor knew where the documents were; the bomber didn't, as you said, Gordon\u2026\" Perkins' words were interrupted by a coarse roar, coming from the workers digging through the cellar debris. They all turned around. [2187] \"What is it?\" Bowlin called, running over quickly. [2188] Three people were bending over something, their heads almost touching. One of them turned around, pale and trembling. He managed to speak: \"Sir, there's a body here. From the look on his face, he was \u2013 murdered.\" [2189] The young man ran towards the edge of the house foundation, stepping on black ash. Reen followed slowly, his face pale and anxious. [2190] Roarke looked at the scene in front of him and roughly pushed Perkins aside. \"Petty, get away from here,\" he said hoarsely. \"You'd better go stand under that tree over there. This \u2013 it's not good.\" [2191] \"Oh,\" Perkins said. Her nostrils flared, and she silently obeyed his request. [2192] The men all widened their eyes and focused intently on the deep hole. A young, red-faced policeman crawled out from the corner of the cellar, bent over, and began to shake and vomit... The body was badly burned, almost unrecognizable - one leg and one arm were missing, and the clothes were completely burnt. [2193] \"How do you know he was murdered?\" Reen asked sternly. [2194] An older policeman's mouth twisted: \"He's not completely burnt, I can see the hole.\" [2195] \"Hole?\" Rowe croaked. [2196] The man sighed. \"Three holes. In his stomach, clean and neat. Those are bullet holes, sir, don't forget.\" [2197] Three hours later, Raine, Detective Bolling, Peachins, and the young Roe sat silently in the office of the Prosecutor of Tarrytown. Someone had called to dispatch a car to take the body to the morgue in Tarrytown. Apart 