e that he died more than thirty-six hours ago, so he must have died around midnight on Sunday.\" [2207] \"Midnight on Sunday!\" Percy glared at Roe, who glared back at her. Mr. Jury Ring shuddered. [2208] \"This is consistent with the time on the watch,\" the prosecutor concluded. \"Twelve o'clock - twenty - six minutes. The watch must have stopped at the time of the murder. He was killed at zero point twenty-six on Monday.\" [2209] The little bald man continued, \"He was shot from the front, at close range. Three bullets.\" He dropped three deformed bullets on the table. \"The slash on the case is strange. There is also a deep cut on the wrist. The beginning of the cut on the wrist is exactly where the slash on the case stops.\" [2210] \"In other words,\" Roe asked, \"you think the same attack caused the cuts on both the wrist and the case?\" [2211] \"That's the standard answer!\" [2212] \"This is our axe-wielder,\" Roe's eyes blazed with anger, \"or at least the person who used the axe... Doctor, could these cuts have been made by an axe?\" [2213] \"Yes, it couldn't have been a knife. The blade of the weapon must have been wide and had a handle.\" [2214] \"That settles it,\" Pauling said. \"Someone used an axe on this guy, hitting his wrist, smashing his watch, stopping it, and injuring his wrist. And, I think, the killer fed him lead pellets.\" [2215] \"There's one more thing.\" The coroner pulled a small key wrapped in a tissue from his pocket. \"Pauling, your men just brought it over, they found it in a fragment of a pants pocket near the body. Someone has identified it\u2014\"\n[2216] \"Maxwell?\" [2217] \"The caretaker of the house? Yes, Maxwell identified this as the original key to the front door.\" [2218] \"Original!\" The two young men exclaimed in unison. [2219] \"Strange.\" Pauling said. \"Wait a minute!\" He picked up the prosecutor's phone and dialed the headquarters in Tarrytown. He spoke briefly to someone and then hung up the phone, saying, \"Yes. 