\n[1543] \"If what?\" Perkins asked, as the group rushed towards the elevator. [1544] Lane shrugged. \"Schiller says everything is God's judgment. We'll just have to wait and see! Child, I have complete faith in the order of things arranged by the heavens.\" [1545] Perkins quietly joined the group in the elevator and said, \"What was Dr. Martini's intention in examining the Saxony letter? I've been thinking\u2026\"\n[1546] \"Perkins, don't think about it. He's just doing something interesting, but it's not important at this stage. One day\u2014who knows\u2014maybe it will come in handy.\" [1547] They could feel the excitement in the British Museum. Dr. Jotter's goat-like beard was disheveled as he greeted them behind Shakespeare's bust, looking flustered. \"I'm so glad you came. Today has been quite trying\u2026 Roebling, what happened to your arm? An accident?\" [1548] He hurriedly led them through the reception room to his office, where they saw an odd assortment of people. Dr. Sedlar's tall, gaunt face was flushed, and he paced with furrowed brows; a sturdy policeman stood firmly behind a chair, holding a baton; and a tall, dark-skinned Latin American sat in the chair, his eyes dark and fearful, his clothes disheveled as if he had struggled, and a pale gray hat lay shamefully at his feet. [1549] \"What's going on here?\" Sam Chief stopped at the door and said in a low voice, then a sly smile crept onto his lips, \"Who do we have here?\" [1550] At the same time, two people took a deep breath\u2014Gordon Roebling and the Italian sitting in the chair. [1551] \"Constable Coben, how are you?\" The Chief Inspector greeted the policeman behind the chair in a friendly manner. [1552] The policeman's eyes lit up. \"Chief Inspector Sam! It's been ages.\" He greeted him with a smile. [1553] \"Yes, it has been a while!\" The Chief Inspector replied cheerfully. He stepped forward and stood three feet away from the chair. The person sitting on it lowered their head and sadly lowered their eyelids. \"Oh, Joe, what are you doing in the museum? Did you graduate from the bottom of the barrel? Don't tell me you went to university! The last time I saw you, you were stealing furs. Speak to me when I talk to you! Stand up!\" [1554] Dr. Jotter's voice was annoyed: \"This man ran into the museum a few minutes ago, rummaging through the Saxon room, and was caught by Dr. Sedgwick.\" [1555] \"Really?\" Mr. Philo Vance murmured as he entered the room. [1556] \"We called in this policeman, but he refused to say who he was, how he got in, or what he was looking for.\" The curator complained, \"Oh, I don't know what's going on with us!\" [1557] \"Dr. Sedlar,\" Vance asked, \"what was he doing in the Saxon room when you caught him?\" [1558] The British man coughed. \"The most surprising thing, Mr. Vance. You could say - uh - with his level of knowledge, how could he possibly investigate rare books? But I'm sure he wanted to steal something. As Dr. Jagger described, he was rummaging through the cabinets.\" [1559] \"Jagger's cabinet?\" Vance asked sharply. [1560] \"Yes.\" [1561] \"Refuses to give his name, huh?\" The sergeant's mouth stretched wide, \"Well, we can help with that, can't we, Joe? This sneaky, underhanded fellow goes by the name of Joe Vella, the best pickpocket I know, always stealing, picking pockets, and serving time in prison. Right, Joe?\" [1562] \"I didn't do anything,\" the Italian wailed. [1563] \"Joe, how did you get in here?\" [1564] Silence\u2014\n[1565] \"What's in it for you? Who sent you? Your thick skull couldn't have come up with this scheme.\" [1566] The man licked his lips and looked over everyone with his little black eyes, then called out excitedly, \"Nobody sent me to do this. I \u2014 I just came in, that's all, just to take a look around.\" [1567] \"To look at books, huh?\" Sam snorted. \"You know this kid, right, Coburn?\" [1568] The police officer blushed. \"Oh, Sergeant, I'm not sure. I... I think he's been on his best behavior since you left the station.\" [1569] \"What a world we live in,\" the sergeant sighed sadly. \"Okay, Joe, you better explain yourself, or I'm taking you down to headquarters and giving you a good tongue-lashing.\" [1570] \"I didn't do anything,\" Vella grumbled grumpily, but his face began to turn pale. [1571] Gordon Rowe stepped forward, his injured arm wobbling slightly. He said calmly: \"Chief, I think I can help.\u201d Vera looked at him quickly, looking puzzled, and then studied Rowe's face intently, as if searching for familiar features. \"The Jagger that was stolen on the 1599 was visiting the museum that day!\" [1572] \"Gordon, are you sure?\" Percy called. [1573] \"Absolutely. As soon as I walked into this room, I recognized him.\" [1574] \"Gordon,\" Reen said, \"who is he?\" [1575] \"Sir, I don't know, but he's in that crowd. I swear, he was in the museum that day.\" [1576] Dr. Sedra examined Vera as if he were a specimen under a microscope, then stepped back and faced the curtains of the large windows. [1577] \"Joe, speak up,\" Sam said gruffly. \"What are you and those old folks doing here? Don't tell me you have a license to teach in Indiana!\" [1578] Vera's lips were tightly closed. [1579] \"Alright, kid. Dr. Jotter, may I borrow your phone?\" [1580] The driver, George Fischer, and the red-faced dispatcher arrived in a flustered manner. They joined the silent group, both directing their attention to the person curled up in the chair. [1581] \"Fischer, can you identify this scumbag?\" the officer asks. [1582] \"Of course I can,\" Fischer says. \"He's one of the two guys who got on the bus with the teachers.\" [1583] Vera shouts, \"Bullshit! You set a trap!\" [1584] \"Joe, shut up! Fischer, which one?\" [1585] Fischer shrugs, annoyed. \"I don't remember, sir.\" [1586] The sergeant walked up to Barbie. The bus driver was nervous, rubbing his chin with his hands. \"You should know, Barbie. You must have talked to this weasel. He bribed you to let them on the bus, right?\" [1587] Vera glared viciously at the bus driver. Barbie stammered, \"Y-yes, I think so!\" [1588] \"You think so! Is it or isn't it?\" [1589] \"Yes, sir, it is.\" [1590] \"Which one?\" [1591] \"The second one.\" [1592] \"The nineteenth one!\" Percy whispers to Rowe. [1593] \"Sure? No mistake?\" [1594] Barbie rushes forward, and Vera opens her mouth, letting out a piercing scream from her dark throat. Everyone freezes, watching the two entangle and then the police join in, followed by Sam trying to intervene. [1595] The officer pants, \"Good heavens, Barbie, what's the matter? What's the big deal?\" [1596] Coblen gripped the pickpocket's collar, pulling him backward with a ferocious tug three times. The man's tongue protruded, and his limbs went limp.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1715] The inspector strode back, barking, \"Vera!\" \u2013 the thief immediately tensed \u2013 \"My good child, come with me!\" [1716] \"You want - you want to take me where?\" [1717] \"You'll find out soon enough.\" - the scholars had stopped talking and were looking anxiously and seriously at Inspector Sam - \"Dr. Sedra, are you staying here?\" [1718] \"What did you say?\" the Briton whispered, surprised. [1719] \"We're going to check out Dr. Aless's den.\" The inspector grinned slyly. \"Maybe you'd like to come along too.\" [1720] \"Hey -\" Vera swallowed. [1721] Dr. Sedra frowned. \"I'm afraid I don't understand your meaning.\" [1722] \"I have many things to discuss with Dr. Sedra today,\" Dr. Jott impatiently said. [1723] \"Ah, so,\" Reine suddenly stood up, \"Officer, forget it. Dr. Sedra must not be fond of American enthusiasm after this terrible incident. And, Dr., where do you live? In case we need to find you urgently \u2014\"\n[1724] \"The Seneca Hotel, Mr. Reine.\" [1725] \"Thank you. Let's go, Officer. Percy, Gordon, it seems we can't get rid of you.\" The old gentleman chuckled, \"Ah, curious young people.\" He shook his head thoughtfully and headed towards the door. [1726] Chapter 19\n[1727] The Mysterious House\n[1728] With the reluctant Italian leading the way, Dromio drives the Lincoln away from the main road and into a narrow path between the towns of Ephesus and Tirrano. This little road is made of gravel, with low-hanging branches on both sides. They suddenly emerge from the dull world of concrete into the cool countryside. Birds and insects flutter above their heads, and there is no one around. The path winds and turns, passing through the greenery. [1729] \"Are you sure this is the place?\" Sam asks impatiently. [1730] Vera nods cautiously. \"I believe so.\" [1731] They seemed to be going through endless forests, and everyone was silent and pale. Finally, they were going to see Dr. Alessandri! The past few weeks of confusion were about to be cleared up. They watched the trees rush by anxiously. [1732] The leaves disappeared, and they came to another path - the first exit they had met after leaving the main road a mile away. This path was a driveway, snaking off to the left, through the dull, scrubby bushes, to a place about 150 feet away where there seemed to be a house. From the gaps in the trees, you could see the sloping roofs, broken and ragged. [1733] \"Stop here,\" Vera said. \"This is it. Now I can -\"\n[1734] \"Sit down,\" the sergeant glared at him, then turned to Dromio. \"Don't get nervous, buddy. Be careful now, don't scare anyone. Keep quiet.\" [1735] Dromio handled the steering wheel as if he were holding feathers, slipping the car into a narrow alley next to it. The car crept gently forward, and the alley widened a bit, then a small clearing appeared, and before them stood a dilapidated house, weather-beaten and now a dirty grayish-yellow. The paint was peeling, making the whole building look like a peeled potato. There was a small porch in front of the house, with rotten wooden steps that had sunk in. The door was sturdy, and all the windows had shutters tightly closed. The branches of the trees brushed against the walls. To the left of the house stood an old wooden shed, and about ten feet away from it was a shaky house, apparently a garage, with both doors closed. Phone lines and cables stretched mysteriously from the house and the garage into the wild. [1736] \"What a lovely ruin!\" Percy exclaimed. [1737] \"Shh!\" The sergeant said sternly, \"Alright, Dromio. The rest of you stay here. Joe, when I go to scout ahead, don't get any bright ideas. If you cooperate, I promise you won't suffer.\" [1738] He quickly climbed out of the car, crossed the open ground, stepped onto the porch, and despite his bulky frame, his movements were surprisingly light. The paint on the door was just as weathered as the wall paint, but it was still a sturdy piece of wood. There was a small bell next to the door. He didn't ring the bell, but tried to look through the windows, only to find that the tightly closed shutters didn't allow him to peek inside. He lightly descended the steps and disappeared to the left of the house. Three minutes later, he reappeared from the right, shaking his head. [1739] \"Damn it, it seems like an abandoned house. Alright, let's take a look!\" He strode confidently onto the porch and pressed the doorbell with force. [1740] For a moment - so quickly that it seemed he must have been peering through a crack - a man opened the door and stepped out. As soon as the door opened, a bell rang above it. The old-fashioned device hung above the door, and as soon as the door moved slightly, the spring attached to the bell would vibrate and jump. The man was tall and old, with a pale face covered in wrinkles. His cloudy gray eyes briefly rested on the sergeant, then looked at the sunlit carriage, and then back at him. [1741] \"Sir, is there something you need?\" His voice was shrill, \"Can I be of service?\" [1742] \"Is this the house of Dr. Aleis?\" [1743] The old man eagerly nodded, looking somewhat pleased. He smiled and scratched his head. \"Oh, yes, sir. Do you have news of him? I've been worried \u2013\"\n[1744] \"Ah, I see. Wait here,\" the sergeant walked to the edge of the porch, \"you all better come up. It seems we'll be waiting for a while.\" [1745] The emaciated old man led them through the narrow corridor to a small living room. The interior was cool. The furniture in the room was old and shiny, with an old carpet and old paintings. A pungent odor reminiscent of a funeral home filled the air. The old man busily opened the shutters and curtains, and the room seemed even more shabby and repulsive under the sunlight. [1746] The sergeant coldly said, \"First, we want to know who you are?\" [1747] The old man cheerfully smiled, \"Sir, my name is Maxwell. I take care of the house for Dr. Alley, doing all sorts of chores \u2014 cooking, cleaning, chopping wood, buying things in Tarrytown.\" [1748] \"Just a housekeeper? You're the only one here?\" [1749] \"Yes, sir.\" [1750] \"You said Dr. Alley isn't home?\" [1751] Maxwell's smile turned to a look of caution. \"I thought - you didn't know, did you? I thought you would have news of him, sir.\" [1752] Percy sighed, \"Damn it! Mr. Lane, you're right, he must have had an accident.\" [1753] \"Shh, Petty,\" her father said, \"Maxwell, we need to get some information, we have to find your master. When was the last time -\"\n[1754] Maxwell's cloudy eyes were full of suspicion. \"Who are you?\" [1755] The sergeant quickly produced his shiny badge, an old one he had not returned when he retired, which he used to assert his authority. Maxwell surrendered: \"Police!\" [1756] \"Answer my questions,\" Sam said sternly. \"When was the last time Dr. Allais was at home?\" [1757] \"I'm glad you came, sir,\" Maxwell murmured. \"I was worried, didn't know what to do. Dr. Allais is often out, but this is the longest he's been gone.\" [1758] \"All right, tell me, how long has he been gone?\" [1759] \"Let me think. Today is June 22nd. Oh, it's been over three weeks, sir. That was May 27th... Wait, sir, May 27th was a Monday. That's when Dr. Allais left home.\" [1760] \"The day the museum had its strange incident,\" Sam said quietly. [1761] \"Didn't I tell you already?\" Vera called out. [1762] Mr. Lane glanced around the living room and looked at Maxwell anxiously. He said slowly, \"Why don't you tell us what happened on May 27th, Maxwell? I think this story will be quite interesting.\" [1763] \"Well, sir, Dr. Aless left early in the morning and didn't come back until very late in the afternoon, almost evening. He\u2014\"\n[1764] \"How did he look?\" Rowe asked curiously, \"Excited?\" [1765] \"Yes, sir! Excited. He's usually a very reserved person who doesn't show any\u2014any emotions... Sir, you understand what I mean.\" [1766] \"Did he bring anything back?\" Roe's eyes lit up. [1767] \"Yes, sir. It looked like a book. But he was holding the same book in the morning, so\u2014\"\n[1768] \"How do you know it was the same book?\" [1769] Maxwell scratched his chin. \"Oh, they looked the same.\" [1770] The old gentleman said softly, \"That's all right. He took the 1606 Jagger out on Monday morning and came back with the 1599 Jagger, leaving the 1606 one there. Well\u2026 continue, Maxwell, what happened next?\" [1771] \"Next, sir, Dr. Aless told me before he left, 'Maxwell, I don't need you today, you can go home.' So I prepared his dinner and left\u2014walked down the alley to the road, took the bus back to Tarrytown. I live in Tarrytown, where there's family.\" [1772] \"Just that?\" Sam grumbled. [1773] The man looked dejected. \"I\u2014oh, sir, he told me before I left that he'd leave a package on the hallway the next morning, but he said not to mail it and to take it to Tarrytown and have the post office send it away on Tuesday morning. I came back from Tarrytown on Tuesday morning, Dr. Aless was, of course, not there, but the package was, so I took it to Tarrytown and mailed it as he instructed.\" [1774] \"What kind of package?\" Raine asked eagerly. [1775] Maxwell looked puzzled. \"What? Just a package, flat, I guess\u2014\"\n[1776] \"Was it a book?\" [1777] \"Right! It was shaped like a book, sir. It must have been a book.\" [1778] \"Let's take it one by one,\" the inspector said impatiently. \"Did Dr. Ales come back alone on Monday night? Did you notice anyone peeking around outside?\" [1779] \"Oh, he was alone.\" [1780] \"Did you see a burly Irishman, middle-aged, with a liver-colored face, wandering around? Did you see him?\" [1781] \"No, sir.\" [1782] \"Strange. Where did that damn old fellow hide himself?\" [1783] \"Don't forget, Dad,\" Percy said. \"Maxwell left soon after Alec came back. Maybe Donohue was hiding in the bushes, watching Maxwell leave, and then\u2014\"\n[1784] \"And then what?\" [1785] Percy sighed. \"Who knows? I'll give him a biscuit for that.\" [1786] \"Did you notice the address on the package?\" the young Rowe asked. [1787] \"Oh, yes, sir. This gentleman\u2026\" Maxwell nodded towards Lane, \"mentioned that name? The British Museum. It says New York City, Fifth Avenue and Sixty-fifth Street.\" [1788] \"Brown paper? Address written in blue ink?\" [1789] \"Yes, sir.\" [1790] Sam said, \"Well, that clears up a lot. No doubt the man in the blue hat was Alais. He stole the book, left the one from 1606, and then had it sent back to 1599 the next day by messenger.\" [1791] \"He's got nowhere to run,\" Vera said smugly. [1792] \"Good, good,\" Lane muttered, his eyebrows knitted, \"And, Maxwell, do you remember sending a similar package about two months ago?\" [1793] Maxwell's nerves were frayed by the theft of the book, and he became agitated, saying, \"I-I hope I haven't done anything wrong. I don't know... Yes, sir, I did send a similar package, to Mr. Crabbe, I believe, at the Saxon house on Fifth Avenue.\" [1794] \"Your eyesight's fine, isn't it?\" the sergeant said coldly. \"Well, Joe, your luck's in. You're off the hook.\" [1795] \"It's quite surprising,\" the young Rowe whispered. \"It seems everything revolves around Dr. Allis. He's causing trouble in Britain and inciting ruffians to raid the Saxon library. What kind of trick is this book playing on us?\" [1796] Joe Vera shrugged his thin shoulders, his beady eyes gleaming. He noticed the sergeant looking at him and tried to appear relaxed. The sergeant said gently, \"Joe, be smart and stay out of this. Alright, Maxwell, how long have you been working for Dr. Ales?\" [1797] Maxwell licked his dry lips. \"Oh, about three months. He came to Tarrytown... that was at the end of March... He placed an advertisement in the Tarrytown Times seeking an assistant. I applied and got the job. I know when he arrived because Jim Browning, the real estate agent who rented this house, is a friend of mine. Dr. Ales rented the house, paid six months' rent upfront, no contract, no prying, no character references. Browning said it's the way things are done... so we came here, like that. He... he was always good to me.\" [1798] \"No prying, huh?\" Perkins frowned. \"Next thing you know, we'll find out he's the Prince of Suriya, on a secret visit to the United States. Maxwell, does your charming host have many visitors?\" [1799] \"Oh, no, miss. No one comes... No, I'm mistaken. There was one person.\" [1800] \"Oh?\" Rine said softly. \"When?\" [1801] Maxwell furrowed his brow. \"He left a week before he left - I don't remember the exact day. It was a man, but his face was covered, and it was night, so I couldn't see his face. He wouldn't give his name, insisted on seeing Dr. Alles. I told him someone was waiting in the living room to see him, Dr. Alles was very excited, initially refused to come out. He still came out, went to the living room, stayed there for a while. He left, leaving the guest alone in the living room. He told me - I think he was nervous - that I didn't have to stay overnight, so I left. The next morning when I came back, he was gone.\u201d\n[1802] \"Did Dr. Alles ever mention this person, Maxwell? He never told you about this person?\" Rowe asked. [1803] \"Me? Sir,\u201d Maxwell chuckled. \"No, sir. Not a word.\u201d\n[1804] \"So who's this guy now?\" the inspector grumbled. \"Not this guy, Maxwell?\u201d He squeezed Vella's shoulder with his fat hand. [1805] Maxwell's eyes widened, and he burst out laughing again. \"Oh, no, sir. This gentleman doesn't talk like - like that gentleman! That man spoke like Dr. Alles - I mean, a little like an actor.\u201d\n[1806] \"Actor!\" Mr. J. B. Fletcher's eyes widened, and he chuckled. \"That's why you said so. You mean a Britisher, do you?\" [1807] \"Britisher? Right, sir,\" Maxwell exclaimed excitedly. \"They're both the same.\" [1808] \"Strange,\" Percy said softly. \"Who is this guy, then?\" [1809] Rowe's eyebrows furrowed even deeper. \"Listen, old chap, did Dr. Allais mention anything about leaving when he asked you to go home at 3:27 p.m.?\" [1810] \"Nothing.\" [1811] \"The next morning you came back and found the package, and Alais had already left, did he leave a note explaining where he went, or something like that?\" [1812] \"No. I didn't think much of it, sir, but a few days later, he didn't come back\u2014\"\n[1813] The elderly gentleman concluded, \"Inspector, that's why you couldn't find anything about Alais on the missing persons list of the Grayson squad. If Maxwell had reported Alais' disappearance at the time, you might have caught up with him. What a pity!\u201d He shrugged. \"Now it may be too late.\" [1814] \"Dr. Alais\u2014missing?\" Maxwell stammered. [1815] \"Apparently so.\" [1816] \"What am I to do?\u201d The old man wrung his hands. \"This house, and all the furniture.\" [1817] \"Oh, by the way, Inspector,\" the officer said. \"Furniture. Did Alais have furniture when he rented this house?\" [1818] \"No, sir. He bought second-hand goods in Terri Town.\" [1819] \"That doesn't sound like someone who leaves around hundred-dollar bills,\" Sam thought. \"Obviously, he didn't want to stay long.\" He looked shrewdly at Maxwell. \"What does your master look like? Maybe this time we can get a clear description of him.\" [1820] \"Well, he's tall and quite thin,\" Maxwell scratched his chin. \"I have a photo of him, sir. I enjoy photography, and one day, when he wasn't looking, I took a photo of him.\" [1821] \"Great! Photos!\" Rowe exclaimed. He had been restless in his chair, suddenly jumping up, \"Good man, bring it out! Hurry up!\" [1822] Maxwell shuffled to the back of the house as the group stared at each other. The musty smell seemed to intensify, and Verla's nostrils flared as he lit a cigarette. Lane paced silently, his hands clasped behind his back. [1823] \"Photos,\" Pershing murmured. \"Well, that's just what we need to clear up this mess... we can finally solve this tricky problem.\" [1824] The old servant soon returned with a small photo. Sam snatched it and quickly examined it under the light, exclaiming in surprise. Everyone gathered around.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe didn't know what happened until Donoho escaped and called the police to save him. In the hospital, he read the newspaper and listened to reporters' conversations, and learned about the explosion and that everyone believed the body in the rubble was either Sedley brother. He suddenly realized what had happened. When Hamnet was in the house taking the documents, he must have had a fatal conflict with the third person who was pursuing the documents\u2014this third person must have killed Hamnet to get the documents\u2014and then escaped with the valuable documents. [2503] The British man's voice was angry: \"I immediately realized that there was a third person with the documents. I sacrificed so much... so many years of my life to pursue Shakespeare's original document... I thought it was destroyed, but now I believe it still exists, intact! I have to start over and figure out who killed my brother and get my documents back. If I admit I'm William, the whole plan will be fatally compromised, and I'll be arrested by the police. Once I'm extradited to France and put on trial, the documents may be lost forever. So I decided to say I'm Hamnet. I suspected Dr. Jouet, so I've been nervous for a week.\" [2504] He only learned after he spoke that Percy and Rowe had been robbed by Hamnet when they went to the Hamlet Manor. Hamnet had followed Reine, seen Reine's telegram to Sam indicating that the sealed envelope contained the valuable document. [2505] The Inspector's face was somber, Percy's spirits had plummeted, and Rowe frowned as he paced back and forth. Only Reine sat quietly. [2506] Sam finally spoke up: \"Listen, I don't believe you. I'm willing to believe you're William, but that doesn't prove you weren't the second person in the house that night! I think you're lying. There's no evidence that you didn't escape from where your brother kidnapped you and tracked him to your house to kill him for the documents. I dare say that the third party killed Hamnett and took the documents \u2013 it was all a smokescreen \u2013 I don't believe there was any third party!\" [2507] William Sedley's face turned green and white. \"Oh, I \u2013\" He was speechless with shock. [2508] \"No, dad,\" Percy looked exhausted, \"you're wrong. Mr. Sedley didn't kill his brother. I can prove it.\" [2509] \"Oh?\" Lennon raised an eyebrow. \"Is that so, Percy?\" [2510] \"Now that we know he's William, since the deceased is one of the Sedley brothers, the deceased must be Hamnett. The question is: was Hamnett the first or second person to enter the house on the night of the murder? We know that the first person locked Maxwell in the garage and had to take the house key to return to the house. So, when the first person arrived, there was no key to the house. But Hamnett Sedley arrived with a key \u2013 the original we found on his brother William's body \u2013 so Hamnett must have been the second person.\" [2511] \"Hamnett was the second visitor, so he was killed by the first visitor, according to Maxwell's testimony about the bell. Who was the first person? The masked man?\" Percy said excitedly. \"We proved a long time ago that the first person was the ax-wielding killer, so Hamnett must have been killed by the ax-wielding killer. Like Dad just said, could William be the ax-wielding killer? I say no. Because William knows better than anyone where the secret cabinet is, and he doesn't need to chop it to pieces in any situation! So, I say William Sedgwick is not the ax-wielding killer, and he wasn't in the house that night, didn't kill his brother, and there's a third person in this case \u2013 the ax-wielding killer, who doesn't know where the files are, kills Hamnett after he takes them out of the hollow wall panel, and then escapes with the files!\" [2512] \"Excellent,\" Rowe said quickly. \"But who is he?\" [2513] \"I'm afraid we have to start from the beginning,\" Percy said, shrugging helplessly. She fell silent, her forehead furrowed tightly. Suddenly, she let out a choked cry, her face turned deathly pale, and her feet wobbled, swaying slightly. Rowe jumped to her side, alarmed. [2514] \"Percy, what's wrong? What happened?\" [2515] The sergeant roughly pushed Rowe aside. \"Percy, are you feeling unwell?\" [2516] Perkins murmured weakly, \"I - I - feel strange. I - I think I'm sick...\"\n[2517] Reen and the British man rushed forward. \"Chief,\" Reen said sharply, \"be careful!\" [2518] Rowe rushed over and caught her knees just as she began to slide towards the floor. [2519] When Sam and Rowe took Perkins away in a taxi to Sam's apartment, Perkins was in a state of hysteria, crying strangely. Mr. Reen and William Siddler remained in the director's office. [2520] \"It must be too hot,\" Siddler grumbled. \"Poor girl.\" [2521] \"It must be,\" Rene said, standing up and towering over them like a pine tree with snow on its branches, his eyes as dark and bottomless as a well. [2522] Sedgwick shuddered and said painfully, \"I guess it's all over now, isn't it? The search always ends. I shouldn't have cared so much\u2014\"\n[2523] \"I understand your feelings, Mr. Sedgwick.\" [2524] \"Oh, I suppose you'll turn me over to the authorities\u2014\"\n[2525] Rene looked at him with an enigmatic expression. \"Why would you think that? I'm not a police officer, nor am I Sam Inspector. I have no connection with the police. Only this small group of us knows about these things. There's really nothing to accuse you of. You've returned the stolen books, and you're not a murderer. \"\u2014The British man's tired eyes glowed with hope as he stared at Rene\u2014\"I can't speak for Sam Inspector, but as a board member of the British Museum, I suggest you resign immediately from the Trustees and\u2014\"\n[2526] The man's shoulders slumped. \"I understand. It seems difficult... I know what I should do, Mr. Raine.\" He sighed, \"When we argued in the Sturford Quarterly, we never thought\u2014\"\n[2527] \"Would there be such a dramatic ending?\" Raine looked at him and murmured, \"Well, goodbye.\" He picked up his hat and cane and left the room. [2528] Dromio was waiting patiently in a carriage on the sidewalk. The old man stiffly sat down in the back seat of the car, as if his joints ached. The car started moving. He immediately closed his eyes, lost in thought, and seemed to fall asleep quickly. [2529] Chapter 30\n[2530] Mr. Raine's Solution\n[2531] The Inspector is not a subtle man, his emotions are blunt and direct, like a squeezed lemon. He has been a father figure to her, always both thrilled and anxious, confused by her. The more he looks at her, the more he likes her, and the less he understands her. She often confuses him, not knowing her emotions. Poor guy, no matter how hard he tries, he can never predict her next mood or figure out the end of her last mood. [2532] In the ups and downs of pain, he is glad to hand over this young woman who suddenly goes into hysterics to Mr. Rowe. Mr. Gordon Rowe had a theoretical understanding of love before he reached this point in his life, and now he bitterly understood what it meant to love a woman. [2533] Perkins's condition remains a mystery, both elusive and unsolvable. When her tears flowed like a broken dam, she wiped them away with a handkerchief from the young man's chest pocket and smiled at him, then returned to her room. She was indifferent to threats or requests. She advised Mr. Gordon Rowe to leave. No, she didn't want to see a doctor. Yes, she wasn't sick, just a little headache. No matter how the Inspector pleaded, she didn't say another word. Mr. Rowe and his future father-in-law exchanged bitter smiles and left - he had already started obeying orders. [2534] Perkins did not appear at dinner. She said a tearful goodbye, not even opening the door. Late at night, the Inspector got out of bed and walked towards her room, feeling his heart beating strangely. He heard sobbing, raised his hand to knock on the door, but helplessly put it down. He went back to bed, and for the rest of the night, he could only bitterly stare at the dark walls. [2535] In the morning, he peeped into her room. She was still sleeping, with tears on her cheeks, her honey-colored hair scattered on the pillow, tossing and turning restlessly, sighing in her sleep. He ate a lonely breakfast alone, then went to the office. [2536] He works irritably according to the daily schedule. Miss Percy does not go to the office. At four o'clock, he curses loudly, grabs his hat, tells Miss Brodie to pack up and go home, and returns to the apartment. [2537] \"Patty!\" He calls anxiously at the door. [2538] He hears footsteps inside her room and quickly walks through the living room. She stands in front of the closed door, pale and strange, wearing a serious suit and a dark headscarf on her head. [2539] \"Going out?\" He kisses her. [2540] \"Yes, Dad.\" [2541] \"Why did you close the door?\" [2542] \"I \u2014\" She bit her lip. \"I'm packing, dad.\" [2543] He sagged his chin. \"Patty! Dear! What's wrong? Where are you going?\" [2544] She slowly opened the door. The inspector saw a full suitcase lying on the bed through a sudden surge of fog. [2545] \"I'm going away for a few days.\" Her voice trembled. \"I \u2014 it's important.\" [2546] \"Why?\" [2547] \"Don't ask, Dad.\" She slammed the box shut, buckled the strap, \"Please don't ask me where I'm going or why. Please. Just a few days. I - I'm going\u2026\"\n[2548] The officer slumped into a chair in the living room, staring at her. She grabbed the suitcase, ran across the room, then held back tears, put the suitcase down, and ran back, hugging his neck and kissing him. By the time he recovered from the shock, she was gone. [2549] He sat weakly in the empty apartment, a cigarette in his mouth, a hat on his head, and the sound of the apartment door slamming shut still ringing in his ears. He sat still for hours in the gradually darkening living room, thinking over and over again, becoming more and more uneasy. He had dealt with crime and police throughout his life, and had a keen insight into human nature. When he forgot that Percy was his own flesh and blood, he could appreciate the odd side of her behavior more. His daughter was a cool-headed, mature woman. She wasn't throwing a tantrum, but why was her behavior so strange\u2026 He sat motionless in the darkening living room for hours. [2550] Two days passed, time crawling by painfully. Gordon Rowe's days were miserable. The young man either called or appeared at strange times in the office, like a stubborn leech clinging to the officer. Sam explained that Percy was 