d that he was looking for something; he didn't know where it was, and he was determined to find it. He was systematically destroying furniture and other things with an axe. Most importantly, this person was not Dr. Ales.\" [2038] Rowe yawned. \"Of course not. If he was Ales, he would have known where to find his own things. The secret cabinet on the wall was definitely his doing.\" [2039] \"Don't be so uninterested, please?\" Percy smiled. \"You're almost tearing your brain apart... I don't know. Your reasoning is correct. The axe-wielder is our unknown variable, and Dr. Ales wouldn't burn down his own place to make firewood, and he knows where the axe-wielder's thing is. But on the other hand, the axe-wielder's thing was found\u2014evidence that the secret cabinet was open, so someone must have opened it.\" [2040] \"So you think there were two people in the house last night? The axe-wielder\u2014such a melodramatic term\u2014why couldn't he find the cabinet himself? He put so much effort into doing something so despicable.\" [2041] \"Alright, you're being clever,\" Percy said. \"You saw it yourself, the cabinet was built so secretly, and Pauling could only open it because he saw that spot and went to feel the rose carving, opening the door. If the door was closed, and the wall was blank, the person looking for something would have to guess the panel, then guess the rose, then know to turn the rose twice \u2013 the odds are a million to one. In other words, that hole couldn't have been accidentally left open! If the ax-wielder knew about the rose and the hole, he wouldn't have gone around chopping everything up. So I say turning the rose lock, opening the cabinet, and taking out what's inside, the person who didn't close the door is not the ax-wielder. If it's not the ax-wielder, then it must be someone else. So we have two people, don't we? Let's see what happens!\" [2042] \"What a female Sherlock Holmes!\" Roe smiled. \"Percy, you're a gem. This reasoning is perfect, and there's a conclusion. The other person \u2013 if there is one \u2013 when did he open the cabinet? That is, was he before or after the ax-wielder?\" [2043] \"Teacher, it must have been after,\" Percy said. \"If this person opened the cabinet first, the ax-wielder would have come later and seen the cabinet door open, and he would have known where the hiding place was immediately. The result would be that he wouldn't have destroyed the house looking for the hiding place... Oh, Gordon, the ax-wielder coming first means he kidnapped Maxwell and kept him in the garage. Then the second person came, and then only heaven knows what happened.\" [2044] They were silent for a long time. Both lay on the grass, looking up at the sky filled with clouds like cotton. Roe's brown hand moved slightly, touching hers. His hand stayed there, and she didn't pull her hand away. [2045] After an early dinner, the three of them came to the British-style old study of Rayne, filled with the smell of leather, books, and wood. Percy sat in the armchair of the old gentleman, picking up a sheet of paper and doodling idly. Rayne and Roe sat at the desk, resting under the dim light of the table lamp. [2046] \"You know,\" Percy suddenly spoke, \"before dinner, I wrote down a few things - hmm, that worried me. I can call them special points.\" [2047] \"Really?\" Ren said softly. \"Child, your persistence is rare among women.\" [2048] \"Sir! That's my main virtue. Should I read my little essay?\" [2049] She took a long piece of paper from her handbag and unfolded it, and began to read aloud clearly, \"One: The person who handed us the sealed envelope with the symbols was Dr. Ales - evidence: his beard and glasses were found in his wardrobe; evidence: he was a 'missing book collector'. Dr. Ales sent Vera to steal the 1599 Jagger from the Saxon family. It was Dr. Ales who sat on the bus rented by the teachers and broke the British Jagger bookcase - Vera's testimony proved this, and the blue cap and gray false mustache found in Dr. Ales' bedroom could further confirm it. But who is Dr. Ales? Is he Hamnet Sedley, as Clara and Vera identified, or is he someone else entirely? Is there any confusion about his identity?\" [2050] \"Two: Who is this Hamnet Sedley? According to Scotland Yard, there is indeed a Hamnet Sedley who was hired as the new curator of the British Museum. But is the person who appeared at the British Museum and claimed to be Sedley really Hamnet Sedley? Or is it, as Dad thought, that someone was pretending to be Hamnet Sedley? He must have something he doesn't want to be known, and he lied about the date he arrived. Did Hamnet Sedley die? Did this person steal his name and take his place? Why did he hide the date of his arrival? What did he do between the actual arrival time and the fabricated arrival time?\" [2051] \"Ah!\" Young Rowe said, \"What a profound thought!\" [2052] Perkins glared at him and continued to read aloud: \"\uff083\uff09If Hamnet Sedley is not Dr. Alessi, what happened to Dr. Alessi? Why did he disappear?\" [2053] \"\uff084\uff09What happened to Donohue?\" [2054] \"\uff085\uff09Who chased me and Gordon and took the envelope?\" [2055] \"\uff086\uff09The axe-wielder is who? He is not Dr. Alessi, but someone else.\" [2056] \"\uff08Seven\uff09 Who followed the axeman and opened the secret cabinet? It could be Dr. Aless himself \u2013 he would know where he hid things.\" [2057] \"Wait a minute, Pechins,\" said Lane. \"How do you know the axeman isn't Dr. Aless? And there were two people in the Aless house last night?\" [2058] \"No, there's one more,\" Pechins frowned. \"The most important and puzzling question.\" She continued, \"\uff08Eight\uff09 What do these points point to? There's no doubt that Dr. Aless's 'million-dollar secret' is involved. But the million-dollar secret is related to the symbol Dr. Aless left for my father, so the solution to everything depends on the last question: what does the symbol mean?\" [2059] After she finished speaking, she put down the paper and began doodling on the table. The two men were silent for a while.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nYou must have heard the sound of the glass breaking, right?\" [519] Rowley shook his head dejectedly. \"But Sergeant, I didn't hear it. You see, the door from the Saxon room to the reading room was closed, and I'm usually very focused on my work. Even if a bomb exploded under my chair, I wouldn't even blink an eye. So, the time of the accident could have been any time yesterday afternoon.\" [520] \"Hmm.\" The sergeant walked over to the broken glass cabinet and peered inside. \"Did anything get stolen?\" [521] Dr. Jotter smiled warmly. \"Come on, Sergeant, we're not children. Of course, we all suspect that someone might have slipped in - you see, there's another door leading to the main corridor, it's easy to get in here - and stole the three rare books from the cabinet. But look, they're all still here!\" [522] Mr. and Miss Samson looked down at the cabinet with the broken glass. The bottom was lined with very soft black velvet, and three rectangular depressions were neatly inset into the velvet, each containing a book. The books were large and thick, with faded leather covers. The left one had a golden-brown cover, the right one was faded scarlet, and the middle one was blue. [523] \"A worker will come to replace the glass this afternoon,\" the librarian continued. \"Now -\"\n[524] \"Hold on a moment, Doctor,\" Mr. Samson suddenly said. \"You said the worker finished cleaning up this room yesterday morning. Didn't you have a guard on duty here yesterday afternoon? I thought museums had strict guard requirements.\" [525] \"No, Sergeant,\" Dr. Jotter replied. \"During the renovation, we don't need as many people as usual. With Donoho and the doorkeeper Burch, that's enough. Those Indiana teachers were the first outsiders allowed to visit after hours, but we didn't think -\"\n[526] \"Hmm,\" the sergeant said loudly. \"I can tell you what happened, and it's not as simple as you think.\" [527] Percy's eyes lit up, and Gordon Rowe looked puzzled. [528] \"What do you mean?\" Dr. Jotter asked immediately. [529] \"I mean -\" the sergeant said straightforwardly. \"Your guess, Dr. Jotter. Donoho saw the man in the blue hat do something unsavory and followed him. Why did he follow the blue hat? I dare say the blue hat broke the glass on the cabinet, and Donoho saw it!\" [530] \"But why nothing was missing?\" the curator countered. [531] \"Perhaps Donoh scared him away before he took one of the books. You said they were valuable. It's simple \u2014 an attempt to steal!\" [532] Percy thoughtfully licked his full lower lip, staring at the cabinet with the broken glass. [533] \"Why didn't Donoh sound the alarm, Sergeant?\" Gordon murmured. \"If Donoh was chasing the man in the blue hat, why didn't anyone see him go out?\" [534] \"And most importantly \u2014\" Percy's voice was low and solemn, \"Where is Donoh? Why hasn't he come back yet?\" [535] \"I don't know,\" the Sergeant snapped back. \"But I'm telling you how it is.\" [536] \"I'm afraid -\" Perkins' tone was odd, \"that something quite terrible has happened. And, Dad, it's not the blue hat - it's poor old Donohue!\" [537] There was a silence. The inspector began pacing on the stone floor. [538] Perkins sighed and bent down to examine the cabinet. Behind each book in the glass case was a folded card, and in front, a larger plaque: \"William Jaggard's rarest editions.\" [539] \"Elizabethan?\" Perkins asked. [540] Dr. Jotter nodded absent-mindedly. \"Yes. Miss Sam, these collections are quite interesting. Jaggard was a famous printer and publisher in London who printed and published the first complete set of Shakespeare's works. These all come from Samuel Saxon's collection - who knows how he managed to get them from London! He was a notorious skinflint.\" [541] \"I wouldn't say that.\" Rowe's light brown eyes flashed. [542] \"Oh, purely from the perspective of a book collector,\" Dr. Jott added hastily. [543] \"Alright.\" The sergeant sighed impatiently. \"I'll check it out.\" [544] There was much to check, but nothing was found. With Dr. Jott's help, Sergeant Sam questioned every worker in the British Museum \u2013 decorators, painters, masons, carpenters \u2013 one by one about their activities the day before. None of them remembered seeing a man in a blue hat enter or leave the Saxton room, and none could recall Donohue's specific activities. [545] The Perkins, who had been lingering in the Saxton room, talking to the young Rowe, quickly entered the reading room, his face aglow. The sergeant was fruitlessly questioning the workers. [546] \"Dad! I think there's something... You don't mind if I don't go back to the office with you, do you?\" [547] At these words, the sergeant's paternal authority was awakened, and he put on a stern expression: \"Where are you going?\" [548] \"Lunch,\" Percy said cheerfully, stealing a glance at the mirror in his pocket. [549] \"Ha!\" said the sergeant, \"lunch, huh? \" He looked a little sad. [550] \"I guess it's with young Roe,\" Dr. Jotter grinned, \"that young man is incorrigibly frivolous for such a serious literary theme. Ah, here he comes.\" As he spoke, Roe came in with his hat and cane, \"Roe, are you coming back this afternoon?\" [551] \"If I can get away,\" the young man grinned, \"Shakespeare has been waiting for three hundred years, so I think he can wait a little longer. Chief, you don't mind, do you?\"\" [552] \"Mind? Mind?\" the Chief barked, \"What do I have to mind?\u201d He kissed Percy's forehead hard. [553] The young couple left the room briskly, chatting animatedly, as if the conversation had begun in ancient times and might continue until eternity, leaving silence behind. [554] \"Ah, I should go too,\" the Chief sighed, \"Just keep your eyes open. If you hear anything about Donohue or if he comes to you, give me a call.\" He handed the librarian a business card, weakly shook his hand, and stumbled out of the reading room. [555] Dr. Jotter watched his broad back thoughtfully, tapping his lips with the business card and whistling softly, before returning to the Saxon room. [556] Chapter 6\n[557] SOS\n[558] \"I always thought,\" Perkins stared at the grapefruit on the table, \"that students of literature were like chemists who do research - all hunched over, skinny, and dry, with wild eyes, and completely lacking in sexual appeal. Are you an exception, or did I miss something?\" [559] \"I missed something.\" Rowe emphasized, and swallowed a bite of fruit. [560] \"I noticed that the lack of spirit didn't affect your appetite.\" [561]\u201cWho said it was spiritual?\u201d\n[562]The waiter took away the empty plate and brought two bowls of soup. [563]\u201cWhat a wonderful day!\u201d Pechin exclaimed, quickly taking a sip of soup, \u201cYoung man, tell me something about yourself\u2026 Bring the appetizers over, please? I mean, tell me like a private biography.\u201d\n[564]\u201cI\u2019d rather make cocktails. George knows me here, but even if he didn\u2019t, it wouldn\u2019t make any difference. George, two martinis, extra dry.\u201d\n[565]\u201cShakespeare and martinis!\u201d Pechin whispered, chuckling, \u201cFresh! That\u2019s why you\u2019re a scholar and yet still have those mundane habits. You pour alcohol on dusty pages, and the books catch fire, right?\u201d\n[566] \"Like the devil,\" the young Mr. Rowe smiled, \"In fact, I find it annoying to dine with smart and clever women.\" [567] \"Oh, I like that,\" Percy grumbled, \"You arrogant man! I have a master's degree in literature, and I'll show you!\" [568] \"Hardy? Hardy?\" the young man asked, wrinkling his nose, \"Oh, that rhyming guy!\" [569] \"What are you talking about? How am I pretending to be smart?\" [570] \"The very essence of Shakespeare himself. My dear lady, if you truly appreciate Shakespeare, you would know that his poetry does not need external stimulation; it radiates light and heat on its own.\" [571] \"I've learned my lesson, I've learned my lesson,\" Percy whispered, \"Thank you, sir. I'll never forget this little aesthetics lesson.\" Her cheeks flushed red, and she viciously tore a small loaf of bread in half. [572] He tossed his head back and laughed. George, who was carrying a tray with two amber-colored glasses, jumped at his laughter. \"Oh, heavens!\" Rowe laughed, out of breath, \"She can't take it! I think we're both a bit mad... Ah, George, put it down, boy... Cheers, Miss Sam?\" [573] \"Miss Sam?\" [574] \"Darling!\" [575] \"Call me Percy, Mr. Rowe.\" [576] \"Good, just call me Peachins!\" They clinked their glasses solemnly, their eyes meeting at the rim of their glasses, both laughing heartily and choking on their cocktails. \"Now we're writing a memoir. My name is Gordon Rowe, almost 28 by Christmas, an orphan. My income is pitifully small. I think the Yankees are really terrible this year, I know Harvard bought a fantastic quarterback. And, if I keep looking at you, I'll want to kiss you.\" [577] \"You're a very strange young man.\" Peachins blushed deeply. \"No, no, that doesn't mean I accept, you better let go of my hand, the two old ladies at the next table are looking at you disapprovingly... oh, I'm mortified! Just because I heard the word 'kiss', I turned as red as a schoolgirl! Are you always so flippant? I'd rather hear a discussion about Milton's rhyme scheme or the species of butterflies.\" [578] He looked at her, the smile fading from his face. \"You're wonderful.\" He poked at the meatloaf on his plate, silent for a moment. When he looked up, both of them were looking at each other seriously, and Peachins' gaze broke first. \"Honestly, Petty - I'm glad you let me call you that - this childish vulgarity is my escape. I know it's not very smart. I've always been clumsy in social situations. My best years of life were spent pursuing a better education; these years, I've been trying to do something monumental in the field of literary research. You know, my ambitions are huge.\" [579] \"Ambition never harms a young man.\" Peachins murmured softly. [580] \"Thank you for being so understanding, Miss. But, I'm not very creative. Research work really appeals to me. I think I should have gone into biochemistry or astrophysics.\" [581] Penshurst concentrated on her salad, fiddling with a fresh, green celery leaf for a while. \"I really - oh, how stupid.\" [582] He leaned forward, taking her hand. \"Petty, scold me!\" [583] \"Mr. Roe, they're watching!\" Penshurst said, but didn't withdraw her hand. [584] \"Call me Gordon.\" [585] \"Gordon... You hurt me,\" Penshurst said sadly, \"I know you're joking, but actually, Mr. Roe - well, Gordon - I look down on most ignorant women.\" [586] \"I'm sorry.\" He said regretfully. \"My joke was terrible.\" [587] \"No, not that, Gordon. I made some terrible jokes too. I never knew what I really wanted to do, and you -\" She smiled. \"Of course, it sounds ridiculous. But the only difference between us and lower animals is our ability to think, and I don't understand why women, just because they're different physically from men, should give up cultivating their minds.\" [588] \"The thought of it is terrifying, it's fashionable.\" [589] \"I know. I hate this fashion. Until I met Mr. Philo Vance, I think my seven senses were still closed. He - oh, he makes you want to think, to learn, and he's still a very charming old gentleman... Ah, we digressed.\" [590] \"There's really nothing to say.\" He shrugged his broad shoulders. \"Just work, eat, exercise, sleep, of course work is the most important part. There's something special about Shakespeare that attracts me. There's never been such a genius. What I find profound is not the beautiful words and philosophical ideas spoken by Hamlet or Lear, but Shakespeare himself. What made him? What was his secret? What was his source of inspiration, perhaps just a fire within him? I want to know.\" [591] \"I've been to Stratford (2),\" Pechstein said softly. \"There's something there. In Old Church Lane, near the church, the atmosphere\u2014\"\n[592] \"I've been in England for a year and a half,\" Rowe grumbled. \"The work was awful. Chasing down unrealistic leads, half by imagination. And then, heaven help us\u2014\"\n[593] \"What?\" Pechstein whispered, his eyes shining. [594] He rested his chin on his hands. \"The most important period in an artist's life is his formative years. It's the time of his greatest passion, when all his sensory faculties are at their peak... Yet, what do we know about Shakespeare's youth? Nothing. There's a blank in Shakespeare's story, and if we want to fully and deeply appreciate this artist, we must fill in that blank.\" [595] He didn't open the cigarette case, but put it back in his vest pocket. \"No,\" he murmured. \"It's still too early. I'm not sure. Not sure.\" Then he smiled. \"Patty, let's talk about something else.\" [596] She sighed anxiously, her gaze never leaving him, then smiled at him. \"Sure, Gordon. Tell me about the Saxons.\" [597] \"Well\u2014\" he leaned back in his chair childishly, \"there's not much to say. Mr. Saxon took an interest in me because\u2014well, let's say it was an intuition! I think he saw something in me, he never had any children. Although his character has some flaws, he truly loves English literature. He's a difficult man to please, but he has been supporting my research\u2014promoting me, even letting me live in his house\u2014until he passed away, and I was still working.\" [598] \"What about Mrs. Saxon?\" [599] \"Completely different from him, the stingy woman,\" he said with distaste. \"I shouldn't be ungrateful, but she can be quite unreasonable. She knows nothing about literature, and even less about her husband's collection of rare books. Let's not talk about her; she's a disagreeable woman.\" [600] \"Just because she can't discuss quarto or octavo with you!\" Peckham laughed. \"So who takes care of Mr. Saxon's collection? You?\" [601] \"Now you're delving into ancient history,\" Rowe chuckled. \"This fossil's name is Crabbe. You're getting a fair deal now! Me? My dear Miss, I call him the eagle-eyed, and he certainly has sharp eyes. He's been the librarian for Saxon for twenty-three years, so he's more protective of his things than old Saxon himself. His face darkened, \"Now he's the big boss, Saxon specified in his will that Crabbe should continue to manage his collection. It's going to be even harder to get close to those things.\" [602] \"Can't you work at Saxon's library?\" [603] \"Under strict supervision - not an exaggeration. Crabbe used to manage the books, and now he does the same. These past few months, I've been cataloging items donated to Britain and sorting through some special books. My research hasn't progressed much, but Saxon required it in his will, so there's nothing I can do...you see, Perkins, I must be terribly boring. Tell me about yourself.\" [604] \"Me? There's nothing to say.\" [605] \"I'm serious, Petty. I think you're the most...oh, well, just tell me.\" [606] \"If you insist,\" she fumbled for her mirror in her bag. \"My career can be summed up in one sentence: I'm a bit like a modern guardian of the hearth, a priestess of sorts.\" [607] \"That sounds impressive,\" the young man smiled. \"I don't quite understand what you mean.\" [608] \"I... I've dedicated my life to... certain things.\" She glanced at the small mirror and smoothed her hair. [609] He looked at her intently. \"Improving one's manners?\" [610] She put the mirror away, sighing. \"Oh, Gordon, I don't quite understand myself. I'm sometimes so muddled.\" [611] Rowe says, \"Miss, do you know your fate?\" [612] \"Tell me!\" [613] \"Dear, you're destined for an ordinary life.\" [614] \"You mean... getting married, having children?\" [615] \"Something like that,\" his voice is low. [616] \"How terrible!\" Percy stood up, her pink cheeks flushed with anger\u2014she could feel it, because her cheeks seemed to burn holes, \"Gordon, let's go!\" [617] Inspector Sam returned to the office with a worried look on his face. He greeted Miss Brawdy casually and walked to his room, throwing his hat onto the top of the safe in the corner, and sat down heavily in his swivel chair. [618] He propped his big feet on the table, then put them down, searching his pockets for a cigar, but not finding one, he rummaged through the drawers, finally finding an old pipe. He stuffed a wad of unattractive tobacco into the pipe, lit it, and began to smoke gloomily. He flipped through the calendar, then stood up and paced back and forth, sat down again, muttering curses under his breath, and pressed a button under the desk. [619] Miss Brawdy rushed over, panting. [620] \"Is there a phone?\" [621] \"No, Inspector.\" [622] \"What about letters?\" [623] \"No, Inspector.\" [624] \"Good heavens, shouldn't Tattler have sent the report on the Dulkin case?\" [625] \"No, Inspector.\" [626] \"Damn those squinting eyes! Well, well, Miss Broudy!\" [627] Miss Broudy's eyes widened, and she swallowed, saying, \"Yes, sir,\" and scurried away. [628] He stood for a moment by the window, gazing out at Times Square, puffing furiously on his pipe.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf you have any information on the whereabouts of William Sedley or Dr. Allis, please inform the Bordeaux police. Best regards, hunting successfully. [2243] Trunch\n[2244] \"This explains everything, you see,\" Percy exclaimed, \"As twins, Hamnet and William must be as similar as peanuts in a shell, which is why everyone got them confused.\"\" [2245] \"Yes,\" Raine said softly, \"This news is invaluable. Obviously, Sedley is Hamnet Sedley, Dr. Allis is Sedley's brother William, the fugitive wanted by the French police.\" He interlaced his long fingers, \"But the problem still remains the same. Which corpse was discovered? Hamnet or William?\" [2246] Rowe said: \"And William's attempt to steal the 1599 Jagger in Brie. Mr. Reine, have you heard of this Frenchman Pierre Girouard? In fact, I visited him last year.\" - Reine nodded - \"He is the owner of the second rare book. Saxony is the third, and the other one is unknown. Damaged cover, huh? Nonsense, he was looking for the Shakespeare document!\" [2247] \"Understood, boy?\" the sergeant smiled, \"I'm out of this case now. But we're making some progress, aren't we?\" [2248] \"You want to know -\" Percy suddenly spoke, his fingers idly smoothing his clothes, \"who murdered the man in the cellar?\" They were all surprised, and Percy laughed, \"Oh, I don't know the name. It's like solving a geometry problem, a bunch of unknowns. But there's one thing I'm sure of: the killer is the axe-wielder!\" [2249] \"Oh?\" Rowe rolled back onto the grass. [2250] \"From the evidence provided by the grandfather clock, he was in the study at midnight. At 12:24 he was in the bedroom upstairs - the clock in the bedroom is the evidence. The murder took place at 12:26 - just two minutes later! The killer was swinging an axe, and the deep cuts on the victim's wrist and the watch provided evidence. These can prove it.\" [2251] \"I see,\" said Lane, looking up at the blue sky. [2252] \"Isn't that right?\" Peachins asked impatiently. [2253] But Rene did not look at her lips; he seemed to be studying a strange white cloud in the sky. [2254] \"There's one more thing,\" Roe clearly said, \"The single lens glasses we found in the hallway of the house prove that Dr. Sedra was definitely inside. Was he the killer or the victim? We could easily say he was the victim because the body matches his features\u2026\"\n[2255] \"Unless the corpse is Dr. Aleas.\" Peachings said. [2256] \"But who set the bomb?\" asked the sergeant. [2257] Quincy darted ahead of a man in uniform with a brown face. [2258] \"Are you Sergeant Sam?\" asked the stranger. [2259] \"Yes.\" [2260] \"I'm Officer Bolling from the town of Thayer,\" said the stranger. [2261] \"Oh, right. I called him this morning and told him I was back.\" [2262] \"He told me they found a guy on the road between Ewington and Thayer, kind of simple, looked like he was about to starve to death. A hobo, a little crazy. Wouldn't give his name, just babbling about a blue hat.\" [2263] \"Blue hat!\" [2264] \"That's right. They took him to the Thayer hospital. The chief said you should go see him right away.\" [2265] They saw Bowlin pacing in the hospital's waiting room. He shook Sam's hand warmly: \"It's been a while, Sergeant. Damn, things just keep getting worse. Want to see him?\" [2266] \"Of course I do! Who is it?\" [2267] \"I'm not sure. They just finished taking care of him. He's a sturdy old guy, but he's so skinny you can see his ribs, and it's because he's starving.\" [2268] They followed Pauling down the hallway, getting more and more excited. [2269] Pauling opened the door to a private room. A middle-aged man lay motionless on the bed, with a pile of dirty, ragged clothes on the chair beside him. His face was gaunt and lined with deep wrinkles, his beard unkempt, and his eyes fixed on the wall. [2270] Chief Sam's jaw dropped: \"Donoher!\" [2271] \"Is this the missing Irishman?\" Pauling asked anxiously. (Is this the missing Irishman?\") [2272] Mr. Jules Renet gently closed the door and walked to the bedside, bending down to look at the Irishman. The Irishman's eyes suddenly filled with pain, and he slowly turned his head, staring blankly at Renet's gaze, then at the face of the chief of police, and then his eyes brightened. He licked his lips and whispered, \"Chief.\" (Mr. Jules Renet gently closed the door and walked to the bedside, bending down to look at the Irishman. The Irishman's eyes suddenly filled with pain, and he slowly turned his head, staring blankly at Renet's gaze, then at the face of the chief of police, and then his eyes brightened. He licked his lips and whispered, \"Chief.\") [2273] \"Hello,\" Sam said cheerfully, walking to the bedside, \"you old grouch, you've made us run around in circles. Where have you been? What happened?\" (Hello, you old grouch, you've made us run around in circles. Where have you been? What happened?\") [2274] Donohue's pale face flushed with a hint of red, and he coughed before speaking. \"Well, it's a long story,\" he tried to smile, \"they've been feeding me through a rotten tube. I'd rather trade an arm for a bloody steak. You \u2013 how did you find me, sir?\" (Well, it's a long story,\" he tried to smile, \"they've been feeding me through a rotten tube. I'd rather trade an arm for a bloody steak. You \u2013 how did you find me, sir?\") [2275] \"Donohue, we've been looking for you since you disappeared. Do you have the strength to talk?\" (Do you have the strength to talk?\") [2276] \"Of course, it's an honor,\" Donohue stroked his scruffy face and spoke with a gradually recovering voice. \"Just as I was visiting the British Museum with the Indiana delegation, I noticed a tall, thin man with a mustache and a peculiar blue hat sneaking out of the museum, with something under his arm - it looked like a book. As a man particularly alert to thieves, I didn't have time to sound the alarm and chased after him. He jumped into a taxi, and I followed. We changed various means of transportation, leaving the city and arriving at a dilapidated wooden house near the main road between Therry and Evenston. After a man in black left the house, I ran to the porch. There was a nameplate under the doorbell that read 'Dr. Allis.' I pressed the doorbell, and the man came to answer the door himself. Although he took off his hat and gray fake mustache, Donohue still recognized him. The mustache was a disguise! Donohue was at a loss because he had no evidence that the man was a thief and might have been just his imagination. However, the mustache was definitely gone... He did not have the authority to arrest him, but he accepted a cordial invitation to enter the house and was taken to the study. Donohue mustered his courage and accused the owner of stealing a book from the museum. [2277] \"He was bold enough,\" Donohue's eyes sparkled, \"to admit his guilt. Then he said he would make up for the loss, pay for it, and so on. I took out my pipe and started smoking, thinking I might as well wait until I had the chance to call the police and have him sent to the nearest police station. But I was too nervous and accidentally dropped my pipe on the ground, startling him. So he asked me to leave, and that was it. I walked down the alley, thinking hard, when suddenly my head was hit hard. That's all I know.\" [2278] When he woke up, he found himself tied up in a dark room with a cloth stuffed in his mouth. He had initially thought that Dr. Allis had followed him and attacked him; he had made this assumption until today. He had discovered that the prison that held him was not Dr. Allis's house but a place he had never seen before. [2279] \"Are you sure?\" the inspector said softly. \"Certainly. Dr. Allis's house is destroyed. Continue, Donohue.\" [2280] \"I don't know how long I was unconscious like a dead pig,\" the Irishman's color returned, looking more comfortable. \"What day is it today? Oh, it doesn't matter. Every day, a man with a gun and a mask comes to feed me once.\" [2281] \"Was it Dr. Aless?\" Percy asked loudly. [2282] \"Miss, I don't know. The lighting was poor, and his voice sounded somewhat like... speaking like an Englishman, yes, that's it. I recognize the accent. I've seen many like him back home. Damn him, he always threatened to torture me, curse him!\" [2283] \"Torture?\" Percy asked in surprise. [2284] \"Miss, it was pretty much that. He talked a lot but never acted. He wanted me to tell him 'where the files were,' \" Donohue chuckled, \"I said, 'You're crazy!' He threatened me even more. I don't know what he meant by 'files,' do you understand?\" [2285] \"Strange,\" Rovery said. [2286] \"There were days he didn't even feed me,\" Donohue complained, \"Damn it, I need a leg of lamb to make up for it!\" He licked his lips and continued with the strange tale. Once \u2013 a long time ago, he said, although he couldn't remember the exact date or time, he couldn't keep track of time anymore \u2013 he heard a sound coming from somewhere in the house, a heavy body being dragged on the ground, obviously someone was thrown into a nearby room. Then he heard someone groaning in pain. After a while, he heard the door slam shut. He wanted to signal his neighbor to communicate, as he believed they were both in trouble, but he was tied up and his mouth was gagged, so all his efforts were in vain. For three days, Donohue hadn't eaten and hadn't seen the person who had captured him. After days of painful struggle, he finally managed to break free and forcefully open the door, finding himself in a dark, dirty, and stinking corridor. He listened carefully, but the house seemed abandoned. He tried to find his fellow sufferer, but all the doors were locked, and there was no response after knocking for a long time. He was very weak himself and feared that the person who had captured him would return, so he slipped out of the house and escaped. [2287] \"Can you still find that place, Donohue?\" Inspector Sam asked angrily. [2288] \"Of course, I can't forget it.\" [2289] \"Wait a minute,\" a young man in a white uniform protested at the door, \"this person is still weak, and I strongly object to him going out.\" [2290] \"Who asked for your approval!\" Donohue yelled, trying to sit up again, only to fall back onto the bed with a \"ouch,\" \"I'm not as strong as before. Doctor, give me some more broth, and I'll be able to guide the rescue team.\" [2291] Donohue sat in the front seat of Reen's car, leading the way, while Bowling and the others sat in another car. They arrived at the spot where he had been discovered by the traffic police earlier. Sergeant Sam helped him out of the car, and the brave Irishman stood blinking in the road, looking around. [2292] \"This way,\" he finally managed to orient himself, and the two men got back in the car. Dromio drove slowly, no more than a hundred yards, when Donohue called out. Dromio turned the car into a narrow lane, less than a mile from Aless's house. [2293] The two cars proceeded cautiously. Three houses passed by the window, disappearing behind the road, and Donohue suddenly shouted, \"Here we are!\" [2294] It was an old, dilapidated, and deserted-looking house, more like a shack than a proper dwelling. There was no sign of life, and the entire place was boarded up, looking as if it had been abandoned for years. [2295] Bowling's men quickly cleared the easy obstacles. An old piece of wood served as a battering ram, and the front door quickly gave way like a rotten nutshell. They rushed into the house, drawing their guns. The house was empty and filthy, with no furniture except for the room where Donohue had been held captive. They kicked open one door after another, finally entering a dark and stinking little room with an iron bed, a washbasin, and a chair. A person was tied up on the bed. [2296] He was unconscious. [2297] Paulin's men carried him out into the sunlight, all staring at the twisted, yellowed face of the victim. The same question appeared in everyone's eyes: Was this the starving, filthy victim, Hamnet or William Sedley? The only certainty was that he was one of the two. [2298] Donohue finished his task, emitting a weak groan and collapsing into the arms of the constable. An ambulance that had been following them sped up and took Donohue away. An intern doctor bent over to examine the unconscious Britisher's feeble limbs. [2299] \"He just fainted. Too tight, lack of food, poor air - ordinary signs of weakness. He'll be fine with care.\" [2300] The newly rescued man had a thin, sunken face with delicate golden stubble on his cheeks. The young doctor administered a nutrient injection, and the man's eyes opened, but his gaze was vacant, and he looked dazedly at the constable's shouted questions before closing his eyes again. [2301] The ambulance drove off, and a car pulled up. A young man without a hat jumped out, later to be identified as a reporter drawn to the scene by the popular mystery rumors. Pauling and Sam were stunned by the questions and answered them all. Despite Rayne's frantic winks and gestures, the information was leaked: all about Dr. Allees, \"the French fugitive being pursued by the police,\" Donohue's harrowing story, the confusion over the identities of the Suddle twins...\n[2302] The young man left with a triumphant smile. Rayne coldly said, \"Constable, that was a mistake.\" [2303] Sam blushed. At that moment, a man walked up to Pauling and reported that despite a thorough search, there was no evidence linking the kidnapper to the house. \"I also called the owner of the house in Tirry Town,\" he said. \"He didn't know anyone was living here. He said the house had been 'empty' for three years.\" [2304] He reported that \"I also called the owner of the house in Tirry Town,\" he said. \"He didn't know anyone was living here. He said the house had been 'empty' for three years.\" [2305] He reported that \"I also called the owner of the house in Tirry Town,\" he said. \"He didn't know anyone was living here. He said the house had been 'empty' for three years.\" [2306] The two teams sat silently in their own cars for ten minutes before Gordon Rowe finally said, \"Let's talk about the mystery.\" [2307] \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n[2308] (1) From Shakespeare's play \"Antony and Cleopatra\"\n[2309] Chapter 27\n[2310] Three hundred years of crime\n[2311] Inspector Sam's face was grim. \"The first thing we need to figure out is \u2013 who is he?\" [2312] They arrived at the Teri Town Hospital the next morning, gathering around the Britisher's bed. The chief doctor had called earlier to say that the patient could speak, and careful care, sedatives, and a good night's sleep had worked wonders on him. He had shaved, and there was a glint of animation in his dull face, and his eyes were distant and intelligent. They saw him sitting up in bed, with morning papers spread out on the quilt, cheerfully chatting with the man in the next bed. [2313] The Britisher raised his pale eyebrows. \"What's the question? I don't understand your meaning.\" He looked them over shrewdly, one by one, as if weighing them with a secret scale. His voice was weak, but familiar in tone. [2314] \"I am Dr. Hamnett Sedra.\" [2315] \"Ah!\" said Lane, \"That's great news for Joate!\" [2316] \"Dr. Jotter? Oh, right, Dr. Jotter! He must be worried. Donohue thought I was the man he was tracking, the man in the blue cap. Ha, ha! We look so alike - it's amazing.\u201d He composed himself, \"You know, he's my twin brother.\" [2317] \"So you know he's dead?\" Percy exclaimed. Reen glanced at the detective, who was turning red. [2318] \"I've been surrounded by reporters all morning. Then these newspapers told me everything. Based on the forensic description of the body, it must have been my brother William. You know, he always used a pseudonym, Dr. Alessi.\" [2319] \"Well,\" Sam said, \"Listen, Dr. Sedlar, it seems the case is finally solved. But what the answer is, I know for sure. We've told you - you've got some things that don't add up, and now it's about your brother. We need to know the truth. Since your brother is dead, there's no need to keep it to yourself.\" [2320] Dr. Sedlar sighed. \"I suppose you're right! Well, I'll tell you everything.\" He closed his eyes, his voice barely audible, \"You've made a big fuss about you not telling us when you arrived in New York. I secretly came first to correct a disgraceful behavior of my brother William.\" He paused, and no one spoke; he opened his eyes, \"There are too many people here.\" He suddenly said. [2321] \"Oh, come on, doctor,\" Rowe said, \"we're all in this together, and as for Donohue\u2026\"\n[2322] \"I'm deaf, dumb, and blind,\" the Irishman said with a smile. [2323] He told the story reluctantly.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."